/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cka.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cka", [["cka1",0,1,"An etcd backup on a kubeadm control plane node hangs and then fails with \"context deadline exceeded\":\n\netcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 snapshot save /opt/etcd.db\n\nWhat is missing?",["A --data-dir flag pointing at /var/lib/etcd on the node","A --kubeconfig flag pointing at /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf","The --cacert, --cert and --key flags pointing at the etcd PKI files","Stopping kube-apiserver first so etcd is quiet while the snapshot is taken"],2,"kubeadm's etcd only accepts mutual-TLS clients, so etcdctl needs --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt plus a client cert and key (server.crt/server.key or healthcheck-client). etcdctl does not use a kubeconfig, and snapshots are taken online without stopping the API server.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters",["--data-dir is for etcd itself and for etcdutl restore, not for a live snapshot save; it does nothing about the TLS handshake that is timing out.","etcdctl authenticates with mutual-TLS certificates, not a kubeconfig; --kubeconfig is a kubectl concept that etcdctl does not accept.",null,"Snapshots are taken online from a running etcd; stopping the API server does not quiet etcd and would take the cluster down for no benefit."],2],["cka2",0,1,"You restored a snapshot with:\n\netcdutl snapshot restore /opt/etcd.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restore\n\nThe cluster still shows the old state. What is the next step on a kubeadm cluster?",["Point the etcd-data hostPath in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml at /var/lib/etcd-restore","Run kubectl apply -f /var/lib/etcd-restore on the control plane so the API server loads every restored object","Restart kubelet with --etcd-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restore on every node","Run kubeadm upgrade apply so the API server reads the new data directory"],0,"Restoring only writes a new data directory. The etcd static Pod must be told to use it by changing the hostPath volume (or --data-dir) in its manifest; kubelet then recreates etcd. kubectl cannot read raw etcd data, and kubelet has no etcd flag.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters",[null,"kubectl cannot read a raw etcd data directory; apply expects Kubernetes manifests, not etcd's on-disk database files.","kubelet has no --etcd-dir flag and never talks to etcd; only the etcd static Pod points at the data directory.","kubeadm upgrade apply changes cluster versions; it does not re-read etcd data and would not load the restored state."],2],["cka3",0,1,"You are upgrading the first control plane node of a kubeadm cluster from 1.34.x to 1.35.x. After upgrading the kubeadm package, which order is correct?",["Upgrade kubelet; restart kubelet; kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x; kubeadm upgrade plan; uncordon","kubeadm upgrade node; drain; upgrade kubelet; kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x; uncordon","Drain every node at once; run kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x on each node in turn; then upgrade kubelet on the workers last","kubeadm upgrade plan; kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x; drain; upgrade kubelet and kubectl; restart kubelet; uncordon"],3,"The documented flow is: new kubeadm, plan, apply (upgrades the control plane static Pods), then drain, upgrade and restart kubelet/kubectl, and uncordon. Upgrading kubelet before the control plane would make it newer than the API server, which the version skew policy does not allow.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters",["This upgrades kubelet before running apply, so kubelet would be newer than the control plane, breaking the version skew policy; plan and apply come first.","kubeadm upgrade node is for the other nodes, not the first control plane node, and draining before apply is out of order.","You never drain every node at once, and workers are upgraded after the control plane apply, not before or during it.",null],2],["cka4",0,1,"The first control plane node is already on 1.35. Which kubeadm command upgrades the other control plane nodes and the worker nodes?",["kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x","kubeadm upgrade node","kubeadm join --upgrade","kubeadm init phase upgrade"],1,"kubeadm upgrade apply is run once, on the first control plane node. Every other node runs kubeadm upgrade node, which upgrades its local control plane components (if any) and kubelet configuration.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters",["kubeadm upgrade apply is run only once, on the first control plane node; repeating it on other nodes is not the documented flow.",null,"There is no --upgrade flag on kubeadm join; join adds new nodes, it does not upgrade existing ones.","kubeadm init phase is used during initial cluster creation, not for upgrading already-joined nodes."],1],["cka5",0,1,"A cluster runs Kubernetes 1.33. The team wants to get to 1.35 this weekend with kubeadm. What is the supported path?",["Run kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x directly on 1.33","Upgrade to 1.34 first, then from 1.34 to 1.35","Upgrade the workers to 1.35 first, then the control plane","Rebuild the control plane on 1.35 and join the old workers"],1,"kubeadm supports one minor version per upgrade, so 1.33 to 1.35 must go through 1.34. Workers must never be newer than the API server, so the control plane is always upgraded first.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters",["kubeadm allows only one minor version per upgrade, so jumping 1.33 straight to 1.35 is unsupported and is refused.",null,"Workers must never be newer than the API server, so upgrading them before the control plane breaks the skew policy.","Rebuilding the control plane is unnecessary and risks losing state; the supported path is a normal stepwise kubeadm upgrade."],1],["cka6",0,1,"A new worker must join the cluster, but the bootstrap token from kubeadm init expired days ago. Which command on the control plane gives you a working join command?",["kubeadm init phase bootstrap-token --renew","kubectl create token kubeadm --duration=24h","kubeadm certs renew bootstrap --print-join","kubeadm token create --print-join-command"],3,"kubeadm token create makes a new bootstrap token, and --print-join-command prints the full kubeadm join line with the CA cert hash. kubectl create token issues ServiceAccount tokens, not bootstrap tokens.","Create and manage Kubernetes clusters using kubeadm",["There is no --renew option on kubeadm init phase bootstrap-token, and it does not print a ready-to-use join command.","kubectl create token issues ServiceAccount tokens, which are not bootstrap tokens and cannot authenticate a joining node.","kubeadm certs renew handles certificates, not bootstrap tokens; there is no 'bootstrap' cert or --print-join option.",null],1],["cka7",0,1,"kubeadm init stops at preflight with:\n\n[ERROR FileContent--proc-sys-net-ipv4-ip_forward]: /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward contents are not set to 1\n\nWhat is the right fix?",["Set net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1 in /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf and run sysctl --system","Load the ip_forward kernel module with modprobe, then run kubeadm reset and start again","Add --ignore-preflight-errors=all to kubeadm init and continue","Disable firewalld so packets are forwarded between interfaces"],0,"IP forwarding is a kernel sysctl, not a module. Persisting it in /etc/sysctl.d and applying with sysctl --system fixes the check properly; skipping preflight hides a setting Pod networking really needs.","Prepare underlying infrastructure for installing a Kubernetes cluster",[null,"ip_forward is a sysctl, not a kernel module, so modprobe cannot set it; the value must be written through sysctl.","--ignore-preflight-errors only hides the check; Pod networking still needs forwarding enabled, so the cluster misbehaves later.","Disabling firewalld does not enable IP forwarding; the sysctl stays 0 and the same preflight error remains."],2],["cka8",0,1,"Nodes installed with kubeadm show Pods restarting randomly. kubelet uses cgroupDriver: systemd, but containerd's config.toml has SystemdCgroup = false. What should you do?",["Change the kubelet cgroupDriver to cgroupfs in /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml to match the runtime","Remove the cgroup settings from both files and let them negotiate","Set SystemdCgroup = true in the containerd runc options and restart containerd","Reinstall containerd from the distro package to reset config.toml"],2,"kubelet and the container runtime must use the same cgroup driver. On systemd hosts the recommended driver is systemd for both, which kubeadm already sets for kubelet, so the runtime side is what needs changing. They do not negotiate automatically.","Prepare underlying infrastructure for installing a Kubernetes cluster",["Switching kubelet to cgroupfs is the wrong direction; systemd is the recommended driver on systemd hosts, so fix the runtime instead.","The drivers do not auto-negotiate; leaving them unset does not guarantee they match and can still cause the mismatch.",null,"Reinstalling containerd resets config to defaults, which still ship SystemdCgroup = false, so the mismatch persists."],2],["cka9",0,1,"While preparing Ubuntu VMs for kubeadm, which pair of kernel modules is normally loaded (and persisted in /etc/modules-load.d) for containerd and bridged Pod traffic?",["ip_vs and nf_nat_ftp","vxlan and 8021q","bonding and bridge_ipv6","overlay and br_netfilter"],3,"overlay backs containerd's overlayfs snapshotter and br_netfilter lets iptables see bridged traffic (needed for net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables). ip_vs is only needed for kube-proxy IPVS mode.","Prepare underlying infrastructure for installing a Kubernetes cluster",["ip_vs is only needed for kube-proxy IPVS mode, and nf_nat_ftp is unrelated to bridged Pod traffic or containerd.","vxlan and 8021q are overlay/VLAN modules some CNIs use, not the base modules kubeadm requires on every node.","bonding and bridge_ipv6 concern NIC bonding and IPv6 bridging, neither of which is the required containerd/bridge pair.",null],1],["cka10",0,1,"You plan a highly available kubeadm control plane behind a load balancer at k8s-api.lab.local:6443. Which kubeadm init option makes every kubeconfig and certificate use that address?",["--apiserver-advertise-address=k8s-api.lab.local","--apiserver-bind-port=6443 --node-name=k8s-api","--control-plane-endpoint=k8s-api.lab.local:6443","--service-dns-domain=k8s-api.lab.local"],2,"--control-plane-endpoint sets the shared endpoint that nodes and kubeconfigs use, and it cannot easily be added later. --apiserver-advertise-address is this node's own IP, not the load-balanced name.","Implement and configure a highly-available control plane",["--apiserver-advertise-address sets this node's own IP, not the shared load-balanced name every kubeconfig should use.","--apiserver-bind-port sets the local port and --node-name the node's name; neither writes the LB endpoint into certs and kubeconfigs.",null,"--service-dns-domain sets the cluster DNS suffix (such as cluster.local), which has nothing to do with the API endpoint."],1],["cka11",0,1,"You want to add a second control plane node with kubeadm join. Which extra flags does the join command need so the node gets the shared certificates automatically?",["--apiserver-advertise-address <lb-ip> --token <token>","--control-plane --certificate-key <key>","--upload-certs --discovery-token-unsafe-skip-ca-verification","--node-role control-plane --cri-socket <socket>"],1,"The key comes from kubeadm init --upload-certs (or kubeadm init phase upload-certs --upload-certs), which stores the encrypted certs in a Secret; --control-plane with --certificate-key downloads and decrypts them. --upload-certs is an init flag, not a join flag.","Implement and configure a highly-available control plane",["A token and advertise address let a node join, but without --control-plane --certificate-key it joins as a worker, not a control plane node.",null,"--upload-certs is an init flag, not a join flag, and skip-ca-verification disables CA checks rather than fetching the shared certs.","There is no --node-role flag on join; --cri-socket only selects the runtime and does not download control-plane certificates."],1],["cka12",0,1,"A stacked-etcd control plane has three control plane nodes. How many of them can fail while the cluster can still write to etcd?",["One","None","Two","Three"],0,"etcd needs a majority (quorum): 2 of 3. With one node down the other two still form a quorum; losing two leaves one member, which cannot write. Five members tolerate two failures.","Implement and configure a highly-available control plane",[null,"Zero tolerance is too strict; with 3 members a quorum of 2 remains after one failure, so one node can be lost.","Losing two of three members leaves one, which is below the majority of 2, so etcd can no longer commit writes.","Losing all three obviously stops etcd; three members tolerate only one failure, not three."],2],["cka13",0,1,"What is the key difference between the stacked etcd and external etcd HA topologies in kubeadm?",["Stacked stores data in the API server's memory; external stores it on disk","Stacked needs no load balancer in front of the API servers, while external etcd always needs one","Stacked runs an etcd member on each control plane node; external runs etcd on separate hosts","Stacked uses one etcd member for all nodes; external uses one member per worker"],2,"Stacked is simpler and needs fewer machines but couples etcd and control plane failures. External etcd needs more hosts but isolates them. Both need a load balancer for the API servers.","Implement and configure a highly-available control plane",["etcd always persists to disk in both topologies; it never stores cluster state only in the API server's memory.","Both topologies still need a load balancer in front of the multiple API servers; that is not what distinguishes them.",null,"etcd membership is not one member per worker; both topologies run a small odd number of members for quorum."],1],["cka14",0,1,"kubectl suddenly fails with \"x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid\" on a year-old kubeadm cluster. Which command shows which certificates are affected?",["kubeadm certs check-expiration","kubectl get csr --all-namespaces","openssl x509 -checkend /etc/kubernetes","kubeadm token list --expired"],0,"kubeadm certs check-expiration lists every kubeadm-managed certificate and its expiry. You then run kubeadm certs renew all and restart the control plane static Pods. CSRs are requests, not issued certificates.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters",[null,"CSRs are pending requests for new certificates, not a report of which issued kubeadm certs have expired.","openssl x509 -checkend inspects a single cert file, and /etc/kubernetes is a directory, so it cannot enumerate all certs.","kubeadm token list shows bootstrap tokens, not the control-plane certificates that expired."],1],["cka15",0,1,"Where does kubelet on a kubeadm control plane node look for the kube-apiserver, etcd, scheduler and controller-manager manifests?",["/var/lib/etcd/manifests, set by the etcd --data-dir flag","/etc/kubernetes/pki, next to the cluster certificates","/opt/cni/bin, set by the CNI plugin configuration","/etc/kubernetes/manifests, set by staticPodPath in the kubelet config"],3,"Control plane components on kubeadm clusters are static Pods. kubelet watches staticPodPath (default /etc/kubernetes/manifests) and runs whatever manifests it finds there; editing a file there restarts that component.","Create and manage Kubernetes clusters using kubeadm",["/var/lib/etcd holds etcd's data, and --data-dir has no manifests subdirectory that kubelet reads.","/etc/kubernetes/pki stores certificates, not Pod manifests; kubelet does not run static Pods from there.","/opt/cni/bin holds CNI plugin binaries and has nothing to do with static Pod manifests.",null],1],["cka16",0,1,"Group ops needs read-only access to every namespace, including new ones. The built-in ClusterRole view already has the right rules. Which command grants it?",["kubectl create rolebinding ops-view --clusterrole=view --group=ops -A","kubectl create clusterrolebinding ops-view --clusterrole=view --group=ops","kubectl create clusterrole ops-view --verb=get,list --resource=* --group=ops","kubectl auth reconcile --clusterrole=view --group=ops --all-namespaces"],1,"A ClusterRoleBinding applies a ClusterRole in every namespace, now and in future. A RoleBinding only works in the one namespace it is created in, and there is no -A flag to create it everywhere.","Manage role based access control (RBAC)",["kubectl create rolebinding has no -A flag; a RoleBinding is confined to one namespace, so it cannot cover future ones.",null,"This defines a new ClusterRole but creates no binding, so nobody is granted access; the built-in view role already exists anyway.","kubectl auth reconcile applies RBAC objects from files; it does not create a group binding from these flags."],2],["cka17",0,1,"Team leads should have the rules of ClusterRole app-admin, but only inside namespace payments. What is the least-privilege binding?",["A ClusterRoleBinding that references ClusterRole app-admin","A RoleBinding in payments that references ClusterRole app-admin","A Role in payments that references ClusterRole app-admin","A ClusterRoleBinding with namespace: payments set in its metadata"],1,"A RoleBinding can point at a ClusterRole; the rules then apply only in the RoleBinding's namespace. ClusterRoleBindings are cluster-wide and have no namespace to limit them. Roles hold rules and cannot reference other roles.","Manage role based access control (RBAC)",["A ClusterRoleBinding grants the role in every namespace, which is broader than the payments-only scope requested.",null,"A Role holds its own rules and cannot reference a ClusterRole; roleRef to a ClusterRole belongs on a binding, not a Role.","ClusterRoleBindings have no namespace field to scope them; metadata.namespace does not limit their cluster-wide effect."],3],["cka18",0,1,"An on-call engineer needs to list nodes. You create a Role with verbs get and list on nodes and a RoleBinding in default, but access is still denied. Why?",["The Role must also grant the watch verb before list works","RoleBindings only apply to ServiceAccounts, not to human users","The Role must be created in kube-system, because nodes are system objects owned by that namespace","Nodes are cluster-scoped, so the rule must be in a ClusterRole bound with a ClusterRoleBinding"],3,"Rules for cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PersistentVolumes, namespaces) only take effect through a ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding. list does not depend on watch, and RoleBindings work for users, groups and ServiceAccounts.","Manage role based access control (RBAC)",["list does not depend on watch; the real problem is that nodes are cluster-scoped, so a namespaced Role cannot cover them.","RoleBindings apply to users, groups and ServiceAccounts alike, so the subject type is not the issue.","Nodes are not owned by kube-system; they are cluster-scoped, so a Role in any namespace still would not work.",null],3],["cka19",0,1,"User jane submitted a CertificateSigningRequest named jane with signerName kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client. What must an admin do so she gets a client certificate?",["kubectl certificate approve jane","kubectl create clusterrolebinding jane --user=jane","kubeadm certs renew jane --csr-dir=/tmp","kubectl sign csr jane --ca=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt"],0,"Approving the CSR lets the controller manager sign it; the cert is then in .status.certificate. A binding only gives permissions after she can authenticate, so it is a separate, later step.","Manage role based access control (RBAC)",[null,"A binding grants permissions only after the user can authenticate; it does not approve or sign the pending CSR.","kubeadm certs renew rotates control-plane certificates; it does not sign a user's CSR.","There is no kubectl sign csr command; approval, then the controller-manager signing, is the correct mechanism."],1],["cka20",0,1,"Before a kernel patch on worker node01 you must move its workloads off. The node runs DaemonSet Pods and some Pods using emptyDir. Which command works?",["kubectl cordon node01 --evict-all --force-daemonsets","kubectl delete node node01 --grace-period=60","kubectl drain node01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data","kubectl taint node node01 maintenance=true:NoSchedule --drain=true"],2,"drain cordons the node and evicts Pods. It refuses to continue with DaemonSet Pods or emptyDir data unless you pass those two flags. cordon alone only stops new scheduling, and deleting the node object does not stop its Pods cleanly.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters",["kubectl cordon only marks the node unschedulable and has no --evict-all or --force-daemonsets flags; it moves nothing.","Deleting the Node object does not gracefully stop its Pods and is destructive; drain is the safe way to evacuate a node.",null,"kubectl taint has no --drain flag; a NoSchedule taint stops new Pods but does not evict the running ones."],2],["cka21",0,1,"kubectl drain node02 keeps retrying with \"Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod's disruption budget\". What is happening?",["The Pod carries a toleration for the node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable taint that drain applies","The node has a NoExecute taint that blocks the Eviction API","kubelet on node02 is down, so evictions cannot be confirmed","A PodDisruptionBudget's minAvailable would be broken if this Pod were evicted now"],3,"Eviction honours PodDisruptionBudgets. If evicting would drop the app below minAvailable (for example because other replicas are not Ready elsewhere), drain waits. Scale up or fix the other replicas rather than deleting the PDB.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters",["A toleration for the unschedulable taint is unrelated; the message is specifically about a pod disruption budget.","A NoExecute taint would evict Pods, not block the Eviction API; the error names a PodDisruptionBudget.","If kubelet were down you would see different errors; this message means eviction is being held back by a budget.",null],2],["cka22",0,1,"Which statement matches the extension interfaces in Kubernetes?",["CRI configures cluster DNS, CNI connects kubelet to containerd, CSI stores Secrets","CRI is the API server's storage layer, CNI handles Ingress, CSI runs Pod sandboxes","CRI connects kubelet to the runtime, CNI sets up Pod networking, CSI provisions and mounts storage","CRI signs node certificates, CNI enforces RBAC for kubelets, CSI implements Service load balancing"],2,"kubelet talks gRPC to a CRI runtime such as containerd or CRI-O; a CNI plugin (Calico, Cilium, Flannel) gives Pods IPs and routes; a CSI driver lets storage vendors plug in provisioning and attach/mount.","Understand extension interfaces (CNI, CSI, CRI, etc.)",["This scrambles the roles: CRI does not configure DNS, CNI is not the kubelet-to-containerd link, and CSI does not store Secrets.","Wrong across the board: CRI is not the API server's storage, CNI does not handle Ingress, and CSI does not run sandboxes.",null,"None map correctly: CRI does not sign certs, CNI does not enforce RBAC, and CSI does not load-balance Services."],1],["cka23",0,1,"You applied an operator's CRDs and created a Database custom resource. kubectl get databases shows it, but no Pods or Services ever appear. What is the most likely cause?",["The CRD was created with scope: Namespaced when it should be Cluster-scoped","The operator's controller Deployment is not installed or not running","Custom resources need a ValidatingWebhook before they are stored","kubectl apply must be run with --server-side for custom resources"],1,"A CRD only teaches the API server a new type; the operator's controller watches those objects and creates the real resources. If the object is stored and listed, the API side works and the missing piece is the controller.","Understand CRDs, install and configure operators",["Namespaced vs Cluster scope only changes how the object is addressed; it does not stop a controller, and the object is already listed.",null,"A ValidatingWebhook can reject writes but is not required to store a custom resource; the object was saved, so admission is fine.","--server-side changes how the apply is merged, not whether a controller reconciles the resource into Pods and Services."],2],["cka24",0,1,"You need to install the cert-manager chart at chart version 1.16.0 and make the command safe to rerun in a script. Which command fits?",["helm upgrade --install cert-manager jetstack/cert-manager -n cert-manager --create-namespace --version 1.16.0","helm install cert-manager jetstack/cert-manager --app-version 1.16.0 --replace -n cert-manager","helm pull jetstack/cert-manager --version 1.16.0 && kubectl apply -f cert-manager-1.16.0.tgz -n cert-manager --force","helm install jetstack/cert-manager --name cert-manager --tag v1.16.0 --namespace cert-manager"],0,"upgrade --install installs when the release is missing and upgrades when it exists, and --version pins the chart version. --app-version is not an install flag, a .tgz chart cannot be applied with kubectl, and --name was removed in Helm 3.","Use Helm and Kustomize to install cluster components",[null,"--app-version does not pin the chart version and is not an install flag; --replace reuses a deleted release name rather than being idempotent.","A .tgz is a Helm chart archive, not a manifest, so kubectl apply cannot install it.","Helm 3 removed --name, and there is no --tag flag; the chart version is set with --version."],3],["cka25",0,2,"Only GPU workloads may be scheduled on node gpu1. Which command keeps other Pods off it without evicting what is already running?",["kubectl taint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu:NoExecute","kubectl label nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu --overwrite","kubectl taint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule","kubectl cordon gpu1 --selector dedicated=gpu"],2,"NoSchedule stops new Pods without a matching toleration but leaves running Pods alone. NoExecute would also evict running Pods that do not tolerate it. A label only attracts Pods that ask for it; it never repels others.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)",["NoExecute would also evict the Pods already running on gpu1, which the requirement forbids.","A label only attracts Pods that request it; it does nothing to keep other Pods off the node.",null,"cordon has no --selector flag and would block all new Pods, not just keep non-GPU ones off while allowing GPU ones."],2],["cka26",0,2,"Which command removes the taint dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule from node gpu1?",["kubectl taint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule-","kubectl untaint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu","kubectl taint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu:None","kubectl delete taint gpu1/dedicated"],0,"A trailing minus on the taint (or just on the key, dedicated-) removes it. There is no untaint or delete taint command.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)",[null,"There is no kubectl untaint command; taints are removed by appending a minus to the key or effect.",":None is not a valid taint effect; it neither sets nor removes a taint.","There is no kubectl delete taint subcommand with that syntax."],1],["cka27",0,2,"A Pod has this toleration:\n\ntolerations:\n- key: dedicated\n  operator: Equal\n  value: gpu\n  effect: NoSchedule\n\nNode gpu1 is tainted dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule. Where can the Pod run?",["Only on gpu1, because the toleration pins the Pod to tainted nodes","Nowhere yet: a nodeSelector for dedicated=gpu must also be added to the Pod spec first","Only on nodes that have no taints at all, since values must be empty","On gpu1 or on any untainted node; a toleration allows but does not require gpu1"],3,"Tolerations remove a barrier; they do not attract. To force the Pod onto gpu1 you also need a nodeSelector or node affinity on a gpu label. The usual dedicated-node pattern is taint plus label plus toleration plus affinity.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)",["A toleration permits but never pins a Pod to a tainted node; it does not force scheduling onto gpu1.","A nodeSelector is not required for the Pod to schedule; the Pod can also run on untainted nodes without it.","The toleration lets the Pod run on gpu1 as well, so it is not restricted to untainted nodes.",null],3],["cka28",0,2,"Read this Pod snippet:\n\naffinity:\n  nodeAffinity:\n    requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:\n      nodeSelectorTerms:\n      - matchExpressions:\n        - key: disktype\n          operator: In\n          values: [ssd]\n\nNo node is labeled disktype=ssd. What happens?",["The Pod is scheduled anywhere because no node matches","The Pod stays Pending until a node gets the label","The Pod is scheduled on the node with the most free disk","The API server rejects the Pod because no node matches"],1,"required... is a hard rule: with no matching node the scheduler leaves the Pod Pending. preferredDuringScheduling... would be the soft version that falls back to any node. IgnoredDuringExecution means removing the label later does not evict running Pods.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)",["required affinity is a hard rule; with no matching node the Pod is not scheduled anywhere, it stays Pending.",null,"The scheduler matches the disktype=ssd label, not free disk space; a label mismatch leaves the Pod Pending.","The API server accepts the Pod; it is the scheduler that cannot place it, so it waits rather than being rejected."],2],["cka29",0,2,"The kube-scheduler is down. Which new Pod can still start?",["A Pod with a nodeSelector for kubernetes.io/hostname=worker1","A Pod whose spec sets nodeName: worker1","A Pod with a toleration for every taint","A Pod created by a Deployment with one replica"],1,"Setting spec.nodeName skips scheduling entirely: kubelet on that node sees the Pod bound to it and runs it. nodeSelector, tolerations and Deployments all still need the scheduler to bind the Pod.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)",["A nodeSelector still requires the scheduler to filter and bind the Pod, so it cannot start while the scheduler is down.",null,"Tolerations only affect scheduling decisions the scheduler makes; without the scheduler the Pod is never bound.","A Deployment's Pods go through the scheduler like any others, so they remain Pending until it recovers."],2],["cka30",0,2,"A 3-replica Deployment must never have two replicas on the same node. Which setting enforces that?",["podAffinity preferred, matching the app label, with topologyKey topology.kubernetes.io/zone","nodeAffinity required on kubernetes.io/hostname with operator NotIn","A PodDisruptionBudget with maxUnavailable: 1 on the app label","podAntiAffinity required, matching the app label, with topologyKey kubernetes.io/hostname"],3,"Required Pod anti-affinity against Pods with the same label, per hostname, puts at most one replica on each node. Pod affinity does the opposite (co-location), and a PDB limits voluntary disruptions, not placement.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)",["podAffinity co-locates Pods, and 'preferred' by zone is soft; it does not guarantee at most one per node.","nodeAffinity selects nodes by label, not by the presence of other replicas, so NotIn hostname cannot enforce one-per-node.","A PodDisruptionBudget limits voluntary evictions; it does not influence where replicas are placed.",null],2],["cka31",0,2,"Read this snippet from a Deployment Pod template:\n\ntopologySpreadConstraints:\n- maxSkew: 1\n  topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone\n  whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule\n  labelSelector:\n    matchLabels: {app: web}\n\nWhat does it do?",["Keeps the web Pod count per zone within one of each other, leaving Pods Pending if needed","Allows at most one web Pod in each zone and has the API server reject any extra Pods at admission","Prefers zones with fewer Pods but schedules anywhere if zones are uneven","Moves running web Pods between zones whenever the counts differ"],0,"maxSkew 1 means the busiest and quietest zones may differ by at most one matching Pod. DoNotSchedule makes it a hard rule (ScheduleAnyway would make it soft). The scheduler never moves running Pods to rebalance.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)",[null,"DoNotSchedule leaves extra Pods Pending; the API server does not reject them at admission.","DoNotSchedule is a hard constraint, not a preference; ScheduleAnyway would be the soft version.","The scheduler never relocates running Pods to rebalance; spread constraints only affect new placements."],3],["cka32",0,2,"Which command creates a HorizontalPodAutoscaler that keeps Deployment web between 2 and 10 replicas at about 70% CPU?",["kubectl scale deployment web --min=2 --max=10 --cpu=70%","kubectl create hpa web --target-cpu=70 --replicas=2-10","kubectl autoscale deployment web --cpu-percent=70 --min=2 --max=10","kubectl set autoscale deployment/web cpu=70 min=2 max=10"],2,"kubectl autoscale creates an autoscaling/v2 HPA with a CPU utilization target. kubectl scale sets a fixed replica count and has no min or max.","Configure workload autoscaling",["kubectl scale sets a fixed replica count and has no --min, --max or --cpu options.","There is no kubectl create hpa command with these flags; autoscale is the correct verb.",null,"There is no kubectl set autoscale subcommand; that syntax is invalid."],1],["cka33",0,2,"kubectl get hpa web shows TARGETS <unknown>/70% and the replica count never changes. metrics-server is running and kubectl top pods works. What is the most likely cause?",["The HPA must be in kube-system to read the Metrics API","The Deployment uses the Recreate strategy, which HorizontalPodAutoscalers cannot scale","maxReplicas is lower than the current number of replicas","The web containers have no CPU requests, so utilization cannot be calculated"],3,"CPU utilization is usage divided by the request. Without requests the HPA cannot compute a percentage and shows <unknown>. Since kubectl top works, the Metrics API itself is fine.","Configure workload autoscaling",["An HPA reads the Metrics API regardless of namespace; it does not need to live in kube-system.","The Deployment strategy does not prevent HPA scaling, and <unknown> points to a metrics/requests problem, not the strategy.","If maxReplicas were the limit you would see it capped at max, not a <unknown> utilization reading.",null],3],["cka34",0,2,"A payments Pod with a high PriorityClass is Pending because the cluster is full. Several low-priority batch Pods are running. What does the scheduler do by default?",["Waits until a batch Pod finishes on its own","Adds a new node through the scheduler's autoscaler","Preempts (evicts) lower-priority Pods so the payments Pod can fit","Rejects the payments Pod at admission for lack of capacity"],2,"Higher-priority Pods can preempt lower-priority ones unless the PriorityClass sets preemptionPolicy: Never. Adding nodes is the Cluster Autoscaler's job, which is a separate component.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)",["By default a higher-priority Pod does not simply wait; it can preempt lower-priority Pods to make room.","Adding nodes is the Cluster Autoscaler's job, a separate component; the scheduler itself preempts instead.",null,"The Pod is not rejected at admission; it is accepted and the scheduler preempts to fit it."],2],["cka35",0,2,"A node-exporter DaemonSet runs on every worker but not on the kubeadm control plane node. What needs adding to its Pod template?",["A nodeSelector for node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane=true","A toleration for node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane with effect NoSchedule","spec.nodeName set to the control plane node's name in the DaemonSet's Pod template","hostNetwork: true so it can reach the control plane"],1,"kubeadm taints control plane nodes node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule. The DaemonSet controller respects taints, so the Pods need a matching toleration. A nodeSelector for the control plane would remove it from the workers.","Understand the primitives used to create robust, self-healing, application deployments",["A nodeSelector for the control-plane label would pull the DaemonSet off the workers; DaemonSets already target all nodes.",null,"spec.nodeName pins a Pod to one node and is not how DaemonSets place Pods; it would not add a tolerated Pod to the control plane.","hostNetwork changes the Pod's networking, not whether it tolerates the control-plane taint."],2],["cka36",0,2,"You delete one Pod of a 3-replica Deployment by name. What happens next?",["The ReplicaSet notices only 2 Pods match and creates a replacement","The Deployment scales down to 2 replicas and records a new revision","Nothing until you run kubectl rollout restart on the Deployment","kubelet restarts the same Pod object on the same node"],0,"The ReplicaSet controller keeps the number of matching Pods at the desired count, so a new Pod (with a new name) is created. That is the self-healing loop; to really remove a replica you change spec.replicas.","Understand the primitives used to create robust, self-healing, application deployments",[null,"Deleting a Pod does not change the Deployment's replica count; it stays at 3 and no new revision is recorded.","No manual rollout restart is needed; the ReplicaSet controller replaces the missing Pod automatically.","kubelet restarts containers within a Pod, but a deleted Pod object is recreated by the ReplicaSet, with a new name."],1],["cka37",0,2,"You updated Secret db-cred, which Deployment api uses through env vars. The Pod template did not change. How do you get the running Pods to pick up the new value with a rolling update?",["kubectl rollout undo deployment/api","kubectl replace --force -f secret.yaml","kubectl rollout restart deployment/api","kubectl annotate secret db-cred reload=true"],2,"Environment variables are read only when a container starts. rollout restart adds a timestamp annotation to the Pod template, which triggers a normal rolling replacement. rollout undo would go back to an older template instead.","Use ConfigMaps and Secrets to configure applications",["rollout undo reverts to a previous Pod template; it would not pick up the new Secret and could roll back other changes.","Replacing the Secret does not restart Pods that read it as env vars at startup; the running containers keep the old value.",null,"Annotating the Secret changes its metadata but does not restart the Pods, so env vars are not re-read."],2],["cka38",0,2,"A ConfigMap was created with immutable: true. You now need to change one of its keys. What must you do?",["Delete and recreate the ConfigMap (or create a new one and point the Pods at it)","Run kubectl edit and set immutable: false, then change the key","Use kubectl patch with --force to change only the data field","Change it with kubectl apply --server-side, which skips the immutable check"],0,"Once immutable is true, neither data nor the immutable field can be changed; the only way is a new object. A common pattern is versioned names (app-cfg-v2) with a rollout. Immutable ConfigMaps and Secrets also reduce API server watch load.","Use ConfigMaps and Secrets to configure applications",[null,"Once immutable is true, the immutable field itself cannot be flipped back, so kubectl edit rejects the change.","kubectl patch cannot bypass immutability; the API server rejects any data change on an immutable object.","Server-side apply still honours the immutable constraint; it does not skip the check."],1],["cka39",0,3,"Which statement matches the Kubernetes network model?",["Pods share the node's IP and are told apart only by port numbers","Pods can only reach other Pods through a Service ClusterIP, never directly by Pod IP","Pod-to-Pod traffic across nodes is always NATed to the node IP","Every Pod gets its own IP, and Pods on any node can reach each other without NAT"],3,"The model requires a unique IP per Pod and flat, NAT-free Pod-to-Pod connectivity across nodes; the CNI plugin implements it. Services add stable virtual IPs on top but are not required for Pods to talk.","Understand connectivity between Pods",["The model gives each Pod its own IP; Pods are not distinguished by port on a shared node IP.","Pods can reach each other directly by Pod IP; Services are a convenience on top, not a requirement.","Pod-to-Pod traffic across nodes is NAT-free by design; that is a core requirement of the network model.",null],1],["cka40",0,3,"From a debug Pod, `curl http://10.96.40.12:80` to a Service works, but `ping 10.96.40.12` gets no reply. kube-proxy runs in iptables mode. What is going on?",["The Service is broken, because a healthy ClusterIP always answers ping","Normal: a ClusterIP is virtual and only forwards the Service's ports, not ICMP","A NetworkPolicy is blocking ICMP but not TCP to the Service","CoreDNS is failing, so ping cannot resolve the address"],1,"A ClusterIP is not bound to any interface; kube-proxy rules only translate the declared protocol and port to endpoint Pods. ping is not a valid test for Services. The address is used directly, so DNS is not involved.","Understand connectivity between Pods",["A healthy ClusterIP does not answer ping; the Service still works for its declared ports even though ICMP gets no reply.",null,"No NetworkPolicy is implied; a ClusterIP simply has no interface to answer ICMP, so ping never works regardless of policy.","The address is used directly as an IP, so DNS is not involved; CoreDNS being down would not affect this ping."],3],["cka41",0,3,"What is kube-proxy's job on each node?",["Give each Pod an IP address from the node's Pod CIDR","Program iptables, IPVS or nftables rules that send Service traffic to endpoint Pods","Answer DNS queries for Service names inside the cluster","Proxy kubectl and kubelet requests from that node through to the kube-apiserver on port 6443"],1,"kube-proxy watches Services and EndpointSlices and writes packet-forwarding rules. IP assignment belongs to the CNI plugin and DNS to CoreDNS.","Understand connectivity between Pods",["Assigning Pod IPs from the Pod CIDR is the CNI plugin's job, not kube-proxy's.",null,"Answering Service-name DNS queries is CoreDNS's role; kube-proxy does not serve DNS.","kube-proxy does not proxy kubectl or kubelet API traffic; components reach the API server directly."],1],["cka42",0,3,"On a kubeadm lab cluster on local VMs, a Service of type LoadBalancer stays at EXTERNAL-IP <pending>. Why?",["LoadBalancer Services need an Ingress resource before they get an IP","The Service needs externalTrafficPolicy: Local set before the controller assigns an IP","kube-proxy must be switched to IPVS mode for LoadBalancer Services","Nothing in the cluster implements LoadBalancer, such as a cloud controller or MetalLB"],3,"Kubernetes only records the request; a cloud controller manager or a bare-metal implementation such as MetalLB allocates the address. The Service still works as a NodePort in the meantime.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints",["An Ingress routes HTTP to Services; it does not allocate the external IP a LoadBalancer Service is waiting for.","externalTrafficPolicy only affects source-IP handling and node routing; it does not make an IP get assigned.","The kube-proxy mode does not control external IP allocation for LoadBalancer Services.",null],2],["cka43",0,3,"You created a NodePort Service with nodePort: 8080 and the API server rejected it. What is the reason by default?",["NodePorts must be in the range 30000–32767","NodePort must equal the Service's targetPort","Port 8080 is reserved for the kube-apiserver","Only LoadBalancer Services may set nodePort"],0,"The API server allocates node ports from --service-node-port-range, 30000–32767 by default. The node port is independent of port and targetPort.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints",[null,"nodePort is independent of targetPort; they do not need to match.","Port 8080 is not reserved for the API server; the problem is only that it falls outside the node-port range.","NodePort and LoadBalancer Services both set nodePort; the restriction is the allowed range, not the Service type."],1],["cka44",0,3,"Which command lists the endpoints behind Service web in namespace shop using the current API?",["kubectl get svc web -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.endpoints}'","kubectl describe pods -n shop --selector=service=web","kubectl get endpointslices -n shop -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web","kubectl get ep-slices web.shop --show-labels"],2,"EndpointSlices are labeled kubernetes.io/service-name with the owning Service. A Service spec holds no endpoints, and Pods are not labeled service=web automatically.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints",["A Service spec has no .spec.endpoints field, so this jsonpath returns nothing.","Pods are not auto-labelled service=web, and describe pods does not list a Service's endpoints.",null,"There is no ep-slices resource name or web.shop syntax; the resource is endpointslices with a service-name label."],2],["cka45",0,3,"A NodePort Service must keep the real client IP visible to the Pods. Which setting does this, and what is the trade-off?",["sessionAffinity: ClientIP; each client is pinned to one node for three hours","internalTrafficPolicy: Cluster; traffic is spread evenly but adds one extra hop","type: ClusterIP with externalIPs; the Service can then no longer be reached from outside the cluster","externalTrafficPolicy: Local; nodes without a ready local Pod will not serve the traffic"],3,"With Local, kube-proxy skips the second hop and SNAT, so Pods see the client IP, but only nodes running a Pod accept the traffic. sessionAffinity pins clients to a Pod, not to a node, and does not preserve source IPs.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints",["sessionAffinity: ClientIP pins a client to a Pod but does not preserve the client's source IP for the Pods to see.","internalTrafficPolicy affects in-cluster routing, not external client-IP preservation; Cluster mode actually SNATs and hides it.","Switching to ClusterIP with externalIPs changes exposure entirely and does not preserve source IP as asked.",null],3],["cka46",0,3,"In the Gateway API, which resource does a platform team create to define the listeners (ports, protocols, hostnames, TLS) that app teams attach routes to?",["GatewayClass","HTTPRoute","Gateway","IngressClass"],2,"GatewayClass names the controller implementation, a Gateway is an actual listener set built from that class, and HTTPRoutes (usually owned by app teams) attach to a Gateway through parentRefs.","Use the Gateway API to manage Ingress traffic",["GatewayClass names the controller implementation; it does not define the actual listeners (ports, TLS, hostnames).","HTTPRoutes are attached by app teams and define routing rules, not the listeners; they reference a Gateway via parentRefs.",null,"IngressClass belongs to the older Ingress API, not the Gateway API's listener resource."],1],["cka47",0,3,"Read this HTTPRoute, created in namespace shop:\n\nspec:\n  parentRefs:\n  - name: public-gw\n    namespace: infra\n  rules:\n  - backendRefs:\n    - name: web\n      port: 80\n\nThe Gateway public-gw in infra has a listener with allowedRoutes.namespaces.from: Same. What happens?",["The route is accepted, because parentRefs names the Gateway's namespace explicitly","The route is not accepted, because the listener only allows routes from infra","Traffic reaches web in infra rather than in shop","The Gateway copies the route into infra automatically"],1,"allowedRoutes controls which namespaces may attach routes. With from: Same only routes in infra can attach, so the route's status shows it was not accepted. Changing the listener to from: All or a namespace selector fixes it.","Use the Gateway API to manage Ingress traffic",["Naming the Gateway's namespace in parentRefs does not override allowedRoutes; from: Same still restricts attachment to infra.",null,"Rejected routes carry no traffic; the route does not silently redirect to a Service in infra.","Gateways do not copy routes across namespaces; the route simply is not accepted."],3],["cka48",0,3,"You want 90% of traffic to web-v1 and 10% to web-v2 using the Gateway API. Where do you set this?",["weight on each backendRef in the HTTPRoute rule","replicas on the two Deployments in a 9:1 ratio","A canary annotation on the Gateway listener","trafficSplit in the GatewayClass parameters"],0,"HTTPRoute backendRefs take a weight, so the split is exact and independent of Pod counts. A replica ratio only approximates a split, and GatewayClass has no traffic split field.","Use the Gateway API to manage Ingress traffic",[null,"A 9:1 replica ratio only approximates a split by Pod count and does not give an exact 90/10 weighting.","There is no canary annotation on a Gateway listener in the Gateway API; weighting lives on backendRefs.","GatewayClass parameters configure the implementation, not per-route traffic splits."],1],["cka49",0,3,"On a fresh kubeadm cluster, `kubectl apply -f httproute.yaml` fails with \"no matches for kind HTTPRoute\". What is missing?",["The networking.k8s.io/v1 API group, which must be enabled on the API server","An IngressClass marked as the cluster default","The Gateway API CRDs, which are installed separately from Kubernetes","The ingress-nginx controller, which serves the HTTPRoute kind"],2,"The Gateway API is developed as CRDs outside core Kubernetes; you install its CRDs and then a controller that implements them. HTTPRoute is in gateway.networking.k8s.io, not networking.k8s.io.","Use the Gateway API to manage Ingress traffic",["HTTPRoute is not in networking.k8s.io and is not toggled on the API server; it comes from separately installed CRDs.","An IngressClass is for the Ingress API; it has nothing to do with the missing HTTPRoute kind.",null,"ingress-nginx serves Ingress resources; even installing it would not register the HTTPRoute CRD, which must be applied first."],2],["cka50",0,3,"Two ingress controllers are installed: nginx and traefik. A new Ingress without ingressClassName is ignored by both. What makes nginx pick up such Ingresses?",["Annotate the nginx IngressClass with ingressclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: \"true\"","Set spec.defaultBackend on every Ingress to the nginx controller Service","Delete the traefik IngressClass so only one class remains","Label the namespace with ingress.kubernetes.io/controller=nginx so its Ingresses go to nginx"],0,"An Ingress without a class gets the default IngressClass at creation time. Setting ingressClassName explicitly on each Ingress also works. defaultBackend is for unmatched requests, not for choosing a controller.","Know how to use Ingress controllers and Ingress resources",[null,"defaultBackend handles unmatched requests within a controller; it does not choose which controller an unclassed Ingress uses.","Deleting the traefik class is unnecessary and disruptive; marking an IngressClass default is the intended mechanism.","There is no such namespace label for selecting an ingress controller; the default IngressClass annotation is what applies."],2],["cka51",0,3,"Names under corp.example must be resolved by the company DNS server at 10.0.0.53, while everything else keeps working as before. Where do you make this change?",["Set nameserver 10.0.0.53 in /etc/resolv.conf inside every application Pod through dnsConfig","Add a Service of type ExternalName called corp.example in kube-system","Change the kubelet clusterDNS setting on every node to 10.0.0.53","Add a corp.example block with forward . 10.0.0.53 to the Corefile in the coredns ConfigMap"],3,"CoreDNS reads its Corefile from the coredns ConfigMap; a separate server block for corp.example forwards just that zone (the reload plugin picks it up). Changing clusterDNS would send all lookups, including cluster names, to the corporate server.","Understand and use CoreDNS",["Editing every Pod's resolv.conf via dnsConfig is unmanageable and would not centrally route just the corp.example zone.","An ExternalName Service aliases one name to another; it does not forward a whole zone to a specific DNS server.","Changing clusterDNS would send all lookups, including cluster-internal names, to the corporate server, breaking DNS.",null],2],["cka52",0,3,"Inside a Pod, /etc/resolv.conf shows nameserver 10.96.0.10 and a search line with default.svc.cluster.local. Where does 10.96.0.10 come from?",["The node's own /etc/resolv.conf, copied into the Pod","kubelet's clusterDNS setting, which points at the kube-dns Service ClusterIP","The CNI plugin, which runs a small DNS server on every node and writes its address","The CoreDNS Pod's IP, written by the scheduler"],1,"With the default dnsPolicy ClusterFirst, kubelet writes clusterDNS (the kube-dns Service IP, backed by CoreDNS) into the Pod's resolv.conf. The node's resolv.conf is used only with dnsPolicy Default.","Understand and use CoreDNS",["The node's resolv.conf is used only with dnsPolicy Default; with ClusterFirst kubelet writes the cluster DNS address instead.",null,"CNI plugins do not run per-node DNS servers or write this address; DNS is served by CoreDNS behind the kube-dns Service.","The address is the stable kube-dns Service ClusterIP, not a CoreDNS Pod IP, and the scheduler does not write resolv.conf."],2],["cka53",0,3,"A Pod runs with hostNetwork: true and can no longer resolve Service names like api.backend. What fixes it?",["Set dnsPolicy: Default","Set dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet","Remove the search line with a dnsConfig","Add a hostAliases entry for the kube-dns Service"],1,"Host-network Pods fall back to the node's DNS settings. ClusterFirstWithHostNet tells kubelet to use cluster DNS for them anyway. Default is exactly the node-DNS behaviour that causes the problem.","Understand and use CoreDNS",["dnsPolicy Default uses the node's DNS, which is exactly the behaviour that loses cluster Service resolution.",null,"Removing the search line does not restore cluster DNS; the Pod still points at the node's resolver.","hostAliases adds static /etc/hosts entries; it cannot resolve arbitrary Service names."],2],["cka54",0,3,"A NetworkPolicy must allow ingress to db Pods only from namespace analytics. Nobody has added custom labels to namespaces. Which selector works?",["namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {name: analytics}}","podSelector: {matchLabels: {namespace: analytics}}","namespaceSelector: {matchNames: [analytics]}","namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: analytics}}"],3,"Every namespace automatically has the immutable label kubernetes.io/metadata.name set to its name. A name label only exists if someone adds it, and NetworkPolicy has no matchNames field.","Define and enforce Network Policies",["The name label only exists if someone added it; here no custom labels were set, so this selector matches nothing.","podSelector selects Pods, not namespaces, and there is no namespace label on Pods to match this way.","NetworkPolicy has no matchNames field; namespace selection uses matchLabels.",null],3],["cka55",0,3,"Read this egress rule from a NetworkPolicy:\n\negress:\n- to:\n  - ipBlock:\n      cidr: 10.20.0.0/16\n      except: [10.20.5.0/24]\n  ports:\n  - protocol: TCP\n    port: 5432\n\nWhat does it allow?",["TCP 5432 to 10.20.0.0/16 except the 10.20.5.0/24 range","All traffic to 10.20.5.0/24 and TCP 5432 elsewhere","TCP 5432 only to 10.20.5.0/24","Everything to 10.20.0.0/16 on any port except 5432"],0,"ipBlock allows the CIDR minus the except ranges, and the ports list limits it to TCP 5432. If this is the only egress rule for a policy with policyTypes Egress, DNS and everything else are blocked.","Define and enforce Network Policies",[null,"The except clause removes 10.20.5.0/24 from the allowed range; it does not grant all traffic to it.","The rule allows the whole /16 minus the except range, not only the /24, and only on TCP 5432.","The ports list restricts the rule to TCP 5432; it does not allow everything except that port."],2],["cka56",0,3,"A developer asks for a Service that sends each client back to the same Pod for the life of a session, with no Ingress in front. Which Service field provides this?",["externalTrafficPolicy: Local","publishNotReadyAddresses: true","sessionAffinity: ClientIP","ipFamilyPolicy: SingleStack"],2,"sessionAffinity: ClientIP makes kube-proxy send a client IP to the same endpoint (for up to three hours by default). externalTrafficPolicy controls node-level routing and source IP, not Pod stickiness.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints",["externalTrafficPolicy controls node-level routing and source IP, not stickiness of a client to a Pod.","publishNotReadyAddresses exposes not-yet-ready Pods in endpoints; it does not pin clients to a Pod.",null,"ipFamilyPolicy selects IPv4/IPv6 behaviour and has nothing to do with session stickiness."],1],["cka57",0,4,"A PV has persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain. Its PVC was deleted, and a new PVC with identical settings stays Pending. The PV shows STATUS Released. Why?",["Retain volumes can only be bound again by a PVC with the same name, in a different namespace","The PV needs a StorageClass before it can be bound a second time","The kubelet is still scrubbing the data, so binding is delayed","A Released PV keeps its old claimRef; an admin must clean it up before it can bind again"],3,"Retain keeps the data and the PV but leaves it Released with the old claimRef. The admin decides what to do with the data, then removes spec.claimRef (or recreates the PV) to make it Available again.","Configure volume types, access modes and reclaim policies",["Retain PVs are not rebound by name alone; the stale claimRef must be cleared regardless of the new PVC's name or namespace.","A PV does not need a StorageClass to bind again; the blocker is the leftover claimRef, not a missing class.","Retain does not scrub data, so there is no kubelet cleanup delay; the PV stays Released until an admin acts.",null],3],["cka58",0,4,"What happens by default to a dynamically provisioned PV when its PVC is deleted?",["It becomes Released and keeps its data on the backend until an admin deletes the PV object","It is recycled with rm -rf and made Available for new claims","It is deleted with its backing storage, since StorageClass reclaimPolicy defaults to Delete","It stays Bound to the deleted PVC until the Pod is also deleted"],2,"StorageClasses default to reclaimPolicy Delete, so dynamically created volumes are cleaned up with their claims. Set reclaimPolicy: Retain on the class (or patch the PV) for data you must keep. Recycle is deprecated.","Configure volume types, access modes and reclaim policies",["With the default Delete policy the PV and its storage are removed, not left Released; Released is the Retain behaviour.","Recycle is deprecated and not the default; dynamically provisioned volumes default to Delete.",null,"The PV does not stay Bound after the PVC is deleted; deletion triggers the reclaim policy."],1],["cka59",0,4,"A new PVC using StorageClass local-ssd stays Pending with the event \"waiting for first consumer to be created before binding\". What does it mean?",["The provisioner is broken and cannot create volumes","Expected: volumeBindingMode WaitForFirstConsumer delays binding until a Pod uses the PVC","The PVC's access mode does not match any PV","The namespace ResourceQuota for requests.storage has been reached, so binding is paused for now"],1,"WaitForFirstConsumer lets the scheduler pick a node first so the volume is created where the Pod can use it (important for local and zonal storage). Create the Pod and the PVC will bind.","Implement storage classes and dynamic volume provisioning",["The 'waiting for first consumer' message is expected behaviour, not a broken provisioner; binding is simply deferred.",null,"An access-mode mismatch gives a different error; this message specifically means WaitForFirstConsumer is delaying binding.","A ResourceQuota block would report a quota error, not the first-consumer message."],2],["cka60",0,4,"PVCs without storageClassName stay Pending on a new kubeadm cluster, even though StorageClass nfs-client exists. What makes them use nfs-client automatically?",["Annotate nfs-client with storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: \"true\"","Set volumeBindingMode: Immediate on nfs-client","Label every namespace with storageclass=nfs-client","Rename the class to default so PVCs find it"],0,"PVCs with no class get the default StorageClass, which is chosen by that annotation. The class name default has no special meaning.","Implement storage classes and dynamic volume provisioning",[null,"volumeBindingMode changes when binding happens, not whether a class is picked when the PVC names none.","Labelling namespaces does not select a default StorageClass; the is-default-class annotation does.","The name 'default' has no special meaning; the default class is chosen by the annotation."],2],["cka61",0,4,"A 10Gi PVC must grow to 20Gi. Its StorageClass has allowVolumeExpansion: true. What do you change?",["spec.capacity.storage on the bound PV to 20Gi","The StorageClass parameters, then recreate the PVC","spec.resources.requests.storage on the PVC to 20Gi","The Pod's volume definition, adding sizeLimit: 20Gi"],2,"Expansion is requested on the PVC; the CSI driver resizes the volume and the PV follows. PVCs can only grow, not shrink, and only when the class allows expansion.","Manage persistent volumes and persistent volume claims",["Editing the PV capacity directly is not how expansion works; the request is made on the PVC and the PV follows.","Changing StorageClass parameters and recreating the PVC would lose the data; expansion is done in place on the PVC.",null,"sizeLimit applies to ephemeral volumes like emptyDir, not to expanding a PVC."],1],["cka62",0,4,"Read these two objects:\n\nPV:  capacity 5Gi, accessModes [ReadWriteOnce], storageClassName: manual\nPVC: requests 10Gi, accessModes [ReadWriteOnce], storageClassName: manual\n\nThe PVC stays Pending. Why?",["The PV is smaller than the 10Gi the claim requests","The access modes must be ReadWriteMany to bind statically","storageClassName manual only works with dynamic provisioning","The PVC must name the PV in spec.volumeName to bind"],0,"A PV can bind only if its class and access modes match and its capacity is at least the request. A larger PV can satisfy a smaller claim, but not the other way round. volumeName is optional.","Manage persistent volumes and persistent volume claims",[null,"ReadWriteMany is not required for static binding; both objects already share ReadWriteOnce, so access modes are fine.","storageClassName: manual with no provisioner is used precisely for static PVs; it does not require dynamic provisioning.","spec.volumeName is optional; the bind fails because capacity is too small, not because the PV is unnamed."],2],["cka63",0,4,"A database volume must be writable by exactly one Pod in the whole cluster, even if a second replica is scheduled onto the same node. Which access mode enforces that?",["ReadWriteOnce","ReadOnlyMany","ReadWriteMany","ReadWriteOncePod"],3,"ReadWriteOnce limits mounting to one node, but several Pods on that node can still use it. ReadWriteOncePod (CSI volumes only) limits it to a single Pod.","Configure volume types, access modes and reclaim policies",["ReadWriteOnce limits mounting to one node, but multiple Pods on that node can still write, which the requirement forbids.","ReadOnlyMany is read-only and allows many mounters, so it cannot give a single writer.","ReadWriteMany allows many nodes to write concurrently, the opposite of a single-Pod writer.",null],3],["cka64",0,4,"You create a PV of type local pointing at /mnt/disks/ssd1 on node worker2. The API server rejects it. What is required?",["A StorageClass with a local provisioner","spec.nodeAffinity that selects worker2","The hostPath type set to DirectoryOrCreate","A ReadWriteMany access mode for local disks"],1,"Local PVs must declare nodeAffinity so the scheduler knows which node holds the disk. There is no dynamic provisioner for local volumes in core Kubernetes, so a no-provisioner StorageClass is only optional.","Configure volume types, access modes and reclaim policies",["Core Kubernetes has no dynamic provisioner for local volumes, so a StorageClass is optional and does not satisfy the requirement.",null,"The hostPath type is a different volume kind; a local PV specifically requires nodeAffinity, which the API server demands.","Access mode is unrelated to the rejection; local PVs need nodeAffinity to pin them to their node."],2],["cka65",0,4,"A StatefulSet with volumeClaimTemplates is scaled from 3 to 1 replica. What happens to data-db-1 and data-db-2 by default?",["They are deleted together with the Pods so the storage is freed","They are kept, so the data is still there if you scale back up","They are resized to zero and marked Released","They are bound to db-0 as extra volumes"],1,"By default StatefulSet PVCs are retained on scale-down and deletion (persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy whenScaled: Retain), so scaling back up reattaches the same data. You can change the policy to Delete.","Manage persistent volumes and persistent volume claims",["PVCs are retained on scale-down by default, so they are not deleted along with the Pods.",null,"PVCs are not resized to zero or marked Released on scale-down; they simply remain for reuse.","Each PVC stays tied to its ordinal (data-db-1, data-db-2); they are not reattached to db-0."],1],["cka66",0,5,"Node worker3 is NotReady. You SSH to it. Which commands should you run first?",["kubectl logs -n kube-system kubelet-worker3","kubeadm reset followed by kubeadm join","crictl rmi --prune and a node reboot","systemctl status kubelet and journalctl -u kubelet"],3,"NotReady usually means kubelet is not posting status. kubelet is a systemd service, not a Pod, so its logs are in the journal. Resetting or pruning images destroys evidence before you know the cause.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes",["kubelet is a systemd service, not a Pod, so kubectl logs cannot fetch its logs; use journalctl -u kubelet.","Resetting and rejoining destroys the node before you know the cause; it is a last resort, not a first step.","Pruning images and rebooting wipes evidence and may not fix the underlying kubelet issue.",null],1],["cka67",0,5,"journalctl -u kubelet on a worker shows:\n\nfailed to load Kubelet config file /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml: ... no such file or directory\n\nThe file is there as /var/lib/kubelet/config.yml. What is the fix?",["Make the file name match the --config path kubelet is started with, then restart kubelet","Run kubeadm upgrade apply on the worker so that the missing kubelet config file is regenerated","Delete the node object so it re-registers","Restart containerd so it rewrites the kubelet config"],0,"kubelet's systemd drop-in (10-kubeadm.conf) passes --config=/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml. Renaming the file (or fixing the flag), then systemctl daemon-reload and restart kubelet brings it back. upgrade apply runs only on a control plane.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes",[null,"kubeadm upgrade apply runs on a control plane node, not a worker, and does not fix a mis-named kubelet config file.","Deleting the Node object does not create the missing config file; kubelet still cannot load its config.","containerd does not write kubelet's config file, so restarting it does not resolve the missing-file error."],2],["cka68",0,5,"kubectl returns \"The connection to the server 10.0.0.10:6443 was refused\". On the control plane node, which command shows whether the API server container is running or crashing?",["systemctl status kube-apiserver","kubectl get pods -n kube-system","crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver","docker service ls kube-apiserver"],2,"On kubeadm clusters the API server is a static Pod run by containerd, not a systemd unit. With the API down, kubectl cannot help; crictl talks to the runtime directly, and crictl logs <id> shows why it exits.","Troubleshoot cluster components",["On kubeadm the API server is a static Pod, not a systemd unit, so systemctl status kube-apiserver finds nothing.","kubectl needs the very API server that is down, so it cannot report the container's state.",null,"kubeadm uses containerd, not Docker Swarm, so docker service ls is not applicable."],2],["cka69",0,5,"After you edited /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml, the API server never comes back and crictl ps -a shows no kube-apiserver container at all. Where do you look?",["kubectl describe pod kube-apiserver -n kube-system","/var/log/kube-apiserver.log on the node","kubectl get events -n kube-system --watch","journalctl -u kubelet, for errors parsing the static Pod manifest"],3,"If kubelet cannot parse the YAML it never creates a container, so there are no container logs; kubelet's journal reports the parse error. kubectl needs the API server that is down.","Troubleshoot cluster components",["kubectl describe needs the API server, which is down, and with no container created there is no Pod status to describe.","There is no /var/log/kube-apiserver.log; a static Pod that never started writes no container log.","kubectl get events also depends on the API server being up, so it cannot help here.",null],3],["cka70",0,5,"New Pods stay Pending with no events at all, while existing Pods keep running. Which component should you check first?",["kube-proxy","CoreDNS","kube-scheduler","The CNI plugin"],2,"The scheduler writes FailedScheduling events when it cannot place a Pod; no events at all suggests it is not running. Check kube-scheduler-<node> in kube-system and its static Pod manifest.","Troubleshoot cluster components",["kube-proxy handles Service routing; it does not place Pods, so it would not cause Pending Pods with no events.","CoreDNS resolves names and is unrelated to Pod scheduling and FailedScheduling events.",null,"A CNI problem shows up as ContainerCreating/network errors after scheduling, not as Pending with no events."],2],["cka71",0,5,"You scale Deployment web from 2 to 5. The Deployment shows 5 desired, but no new ReplicaSet Pods are created and no Pods are Pending. Which component is most likely down?",["kube-scheduler","kube-controller-manager","etcd","kubelet on the workers"],1,"The Deployment and ReplicaSet controllers inside kube-controller-manager create Pod objects. If none exist, nothing reached the scheduler; a scheduler failure would leave Pending Pods instead.","Troubleshoot cluster components",["A scheduler outage leaves created Pods Pending; here no Pod objects exist at all, so the controllers are the suspect.",null,"If etcd were down the API server would fail broadly, not silently fail to create Pods for one Deployment.","kubelet runs Pods once they exist; it does not create Pod objects from a Deployment's desired count."],3],["cka72",0,5,"A Pod stays Pending with this event:\n\n0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated taint {node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: }, 2 Insufficient memory.\n\nWhat is the most direct fix that keeps the control plane free of workloads?",["Lower the Pod's memory request or add memory to the workers","Remove the control plane taint so the Pod can run there","Add a toleration for the control plane taint to the Pod spec","Increase the Pod's memory limit so it gets more memory"],0,"The two workers lack allocatable memory for the request. Removing or tolerating the control plane taint would put workloads there. Scheduling uses requests, so raising the limit does not help.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes",[null,"Removing the control-plane taint would allow workloads onto the control plane, which the requirement forbids.","Adding a toleration also lets the Pod run on the control plane, again violating the constraint.","Scheduling uses requests, not limits, so raising the limit does not help the Pod fit on a worker."],3],["cka73",0,5,"You must write the name of the Pod using the most CPU across all namespaces to /opt/top.txt. Which command lists them in that order?",["kubectl get pods -A --sort-by=.spec.cpu","kubectl describe nodes | grep -i cpu","kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu","kubectl top nodes --containers --sort-by=cpu"],2,"kubectl top pods supports --sort-by=cpu or memory with data from metrics-server. A Pod spec has no cpu field to sort on, and top nodes reports per node, not per Pod.","Monitor cluster and application resource usage",["A Pod spec has no .spec.cpu field to sort on, so this command cannot order Pods by CPU usage.","describe nodes | grep cpu shows node capacity text, not per-Pod live CPU usage.",null,"kubectl top nodes reports per-node totals, not the individual Pod using the most CPU."],1],["cka74",0,5,"You need to see how much CPU and memory has already been reserved by requests on node worker1. Which command shows this?",["kubectl describe node worker1 (Allocated resources section)","kubectl top node worker1","kubectl get node worker1 -o wide","kubectl get pods --field-selector spec.nodeName=worker1"],0,"describe node sums requests and limits of the Pods on the node against allocatable. kubectl top shows actual usage, which can be far from what the scheduler has reserved.","Monitor cluster and application resource usage",[null,"kubectl top node shows actual live usage, not the CPU/memory reserved by Pod requests.","get node -o wide shows addresses and versions, not the sum of requested resources.","Listing Pods on the node does not aggregate their requests against allocatable capacity."],1],["cka75",0,5,"An app writes its logs to /var/log/app/app.log inside the container, and kubectl logs shows nothing. What is the cleanest fix?",["Run kubectl logs with --previous to read the file","Mount /var/log/pods from the node into the container","Set terminationMessagePath to /var/log/app/app.log so kubelet copies the file into the log stream","Have the app log to stdout/stderr, or add a sidecar that streams the file to its own stdout"],3,"The container runtime only captures stdout and stderr; those streams are what kubectl logs and node log files contain. A streaming sidecar (for example tail -F on a shared emptyDir) exposes file-based logs.","Manage and evaluate container output streams",["--previous shows the prior container's stdout/stderr, not a file the app writes, so the log stream is still empty.","Mounting /var/log/pods into the container does not redirect the app's file logs into the runtime's stdout capture.","terminationMessagePath is only read on container exit for a termination message; it does not stream ongoing logs.",null],2],["cka76",0,5,"The API server is down, and you need the recent logs of the etcd container on the control plane node. Which works?",["kubectl logs -n kube-system etcd-cp1 --previous","crictl logs <etcd-container-id>, or the files under /var/log/pods/kube-system_etcd-*","journalctl -u etcd on the control plane node","etcdctl logs --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379"],1,"Container logs are written by the runtime under /var/log/pods (and /var/log/containers links) and are readable with crictl even without the API server. kubeadm's etcd is not a systemd unit, and etcdctl has no logs command.","Manage and evaluate container output streams",["kubectl needs the API server, which is down, so kubectl logs cannot reach the etcd container.",null,"kubeadm's etcd runs as a static Pod, not a systemd unit, so journalctl -u etcd has nothing to show.","etcdctl has no logs subcommand; it queries the datastore, it does not fetch container logs."],2],["cka77",0,5,"You need all Warning events in the cluster, newest last. Which command?",["kubectl describe events --all --type=Warning","kubectl get events -A --field-selector type=Warning --sort-by=.lastTimestamp","kubectl logs events -A | grep Warning","kubectl get events --warnings-only -o wide"],1,"Events support field selectors on type, reason and involvedObject, and --sort-by orders them. Events are API objects, not logs, and there is no --warnings-only flag.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes",["kubectl describe events is not a valid command form, and there is no --all --type filter like this.",null,"Events are API objects, not a log stream, so kubectl logs events does not exist.","There is no --warnings-only flag; filtering is done with --field-selector type=Warning."],2],["cka78",0,5,"Node worker2 is NotReady and kubelet logs repeat \"container runtime network not ready\" plus \"failed to connect to /run/containerd/containerd.sock\". What should you check?",["The kube-proxy DaemonSet Pod on worker2","The CoreDNS Deployment in kube-system","The kubeadm bootstrap token for worker2","systemctl status containerd, then start or fix it"],3,"kubelet cannot reach the CRI socket, so the runtime is down or misconfigured. Once containerd is running, kubelet reconnects and the node returns to Ready.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes",["kube-proxy handles Service routing and is unrelated to the CRI socket connection failure.","CoreDNS is a DNS service; it does not explain kubelet being unable to reach the container runtime.","The bootstrap token is only used when a node first joins; it does not affect a running node's CRI connection.",null],2],["cka79",0,5,"Right after kubeadm init, the control plane node is NotReady and the CoreDNS Pods are Pending. The API server and etcd are healthy. What is the usual cause?",["No CNI network plugin has been installed yet","The CoreDNS image cannot be pulled from the registry","kube-proxy has not been started on the node","The admin.conf kubeconfig has not been copied to ~/.kube"],0,"Until a CNI plugin (Calico, Cilium, Flannel) is installed, kubelet reports the network as not ready, the node stays NotReady and CoreDNS cannot start. kubeadm installs kube-proxy itself, and a missing kubeconfig would stop kubectl from working at all.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes",[null,"An image-pull failure shows ImagePullBackOff, not a NotReady node caused by missing Pod networking.","kubeadm starts kube-proxy itself, so it is not the missing piece right after init.","A missing admin.conf would stop kubectl entirely, not leave the node NotReady while the API server is healthy."],2],["cka80",0,5,"Several Pods on node worker1 are stuck in ContainerCreating with \"failed to setup network for sandbox\". Pods on other nodes are fine. What is the likely area to investigate?",["The Service selectors of the stuck Pods","The ResourceQuota in the Pods' namespace","The CNI plugin Pod and its configuration in /etc/cni/net.d on worker1","The kube-scheduler's leader election lease"],2,"Sandbox network setup is done by the CNI plugin on that node. A crashed CNI agent, a missing binary in /opt/cni/bin or bad config in /etc/cni/net.d all cause this. Scheduling and quotas already passed.","Troubleshoot services and networking",["Service selectors affect endpoint membership, not sandbox network setup; the Pods are already scheduled to the node.","A ResourceQuota would block admission earlier; here the Pods reached the node and fail at network setup.",null,"The scheduler lease affects placement, but these Pods are already placed and failing on CNI networking."],2],["cka81",0,5,"Pods on the same node can reach each other, but Pods on different nodes cannot. Services work only when the endpoint is on the local node. What is the most likely cause?",["A default-deny NetworkPolicy exists in the namespace and only allows traffic on the local node","CoreDNS is crash-looping and returning stale records","The Services use ClusterIP instead of NodePort","Node-to-node Pod traffic is blocked, such as a firewall dropping the CNI's encapsulation"],3,"Same-node success and cross-node failure points at the node-to-node path the CNI uses (for example VXLAN over UDP or BGP between nodes) being blocked or misrouted. A NetworkPolicy would block same-node traffic too.","Troubleshoot services and networking",["A default-deny NetworkPolicy would also block same-node traffic, but here same-node works, so a policy is not the cause.","CoreDNS problems affect name resolution, not raw cross-node Pod-to-Pod IP connectivity.","The Service type does not determine whether cross-node traffic works; the pattern points at the node-to-node CNI path.",null],3],["cka82",0,5,"CoreDNS Pods are in CrashLoopBackOff and their logs show \"[FATAL] plugin/loop: Loop ... detected\". The nodes run systemd-resolved. What is the recommended fix?",["Delete the loop plugin line and leave forwarding as it is","Scale CoreDNS to one replica so it cannot query itself","Point kubelet's resolvConf at /run/systemd/resolve/resolv.conf, the real upstream list","Set dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst on the CoreDNS Deployment"],2,"systemd-resolved puts 127.0.0.53 in /etc/resolv.conf, so CoreDNS forwards queries back to itself. Giving kubelet the real upstream file breaks the loop. Removing the loop plugin hides the problem and leaves DNS broken.","Troubleshoot services and networking",["Removing the loop plugin hides the detection but leaves the actual forwarding loop, so DNS stays broken.","Scaling to one replica does not stop CoreDNS from forwarding to 127.0.0.53 and looping back to itself.",null,"dnsPolicy on the CoreDNS Deployment does not change the upstream resolv.conf that causes the loop."],3],["cka83",0,5,"Which one-off command tests cluster DNS from inside the cluster and cleans up after itself?",["kubectl exec -n kube-system deploy/coredns -- dig kubernetes.default","kubectl run dnstest --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- nslookup kubernetes.default","kubectl debug node/worker1 -- nslookup kubernetes.default --cluster","kubectl get --raw /api/v1/dns?name=kubernetes.default"],1,"A temporary Pod uses the normal Pod DNS path, and --rm deletes it when you exit. The CoreDNS image has no shell or dig, and a node debug Pod uses the host's DNS by default.","Troubleshoot services and networking",["The CoreDNS image has no shell or dig binary, so this exec fails and it cleans nothing up.",null,"kubectl debug node uses the host's DNS by default, not the cluster DNS path a normal Pod would use.","There is no /api/v1/dns endpoint; the API server does not resolve names this way."],1],["cka84",0,5,"Pods on node worker4 cannot reach any ClusterIP Service, but they can reach Pod IPs directly. Pods on other nodes are fine. What should you check first?",["The kube-proxy Pod on worker4 (kubectl -n kube-system get pods -o wide -l k8s-app=kube-proxy)","The EndpointSlices of every affected Service","The CoreDNS ConfigMap in kube-system","The Ingress controller's logs"],0,"Service VIPs are implemented per node by kube-proxy; if its Pod on worker4 is down or crash-looping, only that node's Pods lose Service access. EndpointSlices are shared across nodes, so they cannot explain a single-node failure.","Troubleshoot services and networking",[null,"EndpointSlices are shared cluster-wide, so they cannot explain a failure isolated to one node's Pods.","The Pods reach Pod IPs fine, so CoreDNS is not the issue; only ClusterIP (kube-proxy) access is broken on that node.","The Ingress controller is not involved in in-cluster ClusterIP access from Pods on a specific node."],2],["cka85",0,5,"Pods behind Service api are Running, but `kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=api` shows no ready endpoints. The selector matches the Pod labels. What should you check next?",["The kube-apiserver's --service-cluster-ip-range flag","The node's swap setting and kubelet cgroup driver","The Pods' readiness probes, since unready Pods are not served","Whether the Service type should be LoadBalancer"],2,"EndpointSlices mark Pods that are not Ready as not ready, so traffic skips them. A failing readiness probe (wrong port or path) is a common cause. The Service type does not affect endpoint readiness.","Troubleshoot services and networking",["--service-cluster-ip-range sets the VIP range and does not affect whether Pods appear as ready endpoints.","Swap and cgroup driver relate to kubelet startup, not to a Ready Pod being excluded from endpoints.",null,"Changing the Service type does not make unready Pods become endpoints; readiness is the deciding factor."],2],["cka86",0,5,"kubelet on a node reports DiskPressure, and Pods are being evicted. What should you do first on the node?",["Free space, for example remove unused images with crictl rmi --prune and clear large logs","Remove the node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure taint with kubectl so that Pods can be scheduled there again","Restart the kube-scheduler so it ignores the condition","Add a toleration for disk-pressure to every Deployment"],0,"The condition and taint come from kubelet's eviction thresholds and will return until disk use drops. Freeing space on the node lets kubelet clear the condition itself; removing the taint by hand does not solve anything.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes",[null,"Removing the disk-pressure taint by hand does not free space; kubelet re-applies it until disk usage drops.","Restarting the scheduler does not clear a node condition set by kubelet's eviction thresholds.","Tolerating disk-pressure lets Pods schedule onto a full node, worsening the problem rather than fixing the cause."],2],["cka87",0,5,"Node worker5 lost power. Its Pods show Terminating for a long time, and a StatefulSet replica cannot be recreated elsewhere. After confirming the node is really off, what can you do?",["kubectl rollout restart statefulset <name>","kubectl cordon worker5 and wait for kubelet to confirm","kubectl scale statefulset <name> --replicas=0 --force","kubectl delete pod <name> --grace-period=0 --force"],3,"Without kubelet, the API server never gets confirmation that the containers stopped. Force deletion removes the Pod object so the StatefulSet can recreate it; only do this when you are sure the node is gone, or two copies may run. Deleting the Node object or adding an out-of-service taint are alternatives.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes",["rollout restart cannot proceed while the old Pod is stuck Terminating on a dead node; the replica still cannot be recreated.","cordon only stops new scheduling; with kubelet gone it never confirms the Pod stopped, so waiting does not help.","Scaling to zero and back does not clear a Pod stuck Terminating on an unreachable node; force deletion is needed.",null],3],["cka88",0,5,"You need every node's InternalIP on one line for a report. Which command prints them?",["kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=IP:.spec.podCIDR","kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[?(@.type==\"InternalIP\")].address}'","kubectl describe nodes | grep -i externalip","kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.internalIP}'"],1,"Node addresses are a list in status.addresses; the JSONPath filter picks the InternalIP entries. podCIDR is the Pod range, not the node address, and spec has no internalIP field.","Monitor cluster and application resource usage",[".spec.podCIDR is the Pod IP range for the node, not the node's own InternalIP address.",null,"grep -i externalip returns the external address (often empty on-prem), not the InternalIP requested.","There is no .spec.internalIP field; node addresses live under status.addresses."],2],["cka89",0,5,"You are handed a kubeconfig with several clusters, and a task says to work on cluster k8s-prod. Which commands check and switch the target?",["kubectl cluster-info --context-list, then kubectl login k8s-prod to switch","kubectl config get-contexts, then kubectl config use-context k8s-prod","kubectl get clusters, then kubectl set cluster k8s-prod","kubeadm config view, then kubeadm config use k8s-prod"],1,"Contexts bind a cluster, user and namespace; use-context changes the current one in the kubeconfig. Running a task against the wrong context is a classic lost-marks mistake in the exam.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes",["There is no --context-list flag and no kubectl login command for switching contexts.",null,"There is no kubectl get clusters or set cluster command for changing the active target.","kubeadm config manages cluster configuration, not kubectl contexts, and has no use command."],1],["cka90",0,5,"The API server log repeats \"connection refused\" when dialing 127.0.0.1:2379. Which command tells you whether etcd is healthy?",["kubectl get componentstatuses etcd-0 -o yaml","curl http://127.0.0.1:2379/healthz without TLS","systemctl status etcd on the control plane node","etcdctl endpoint health with --endpoints and the etcd --cacert, --cert and --key flags"],3,"etcdctl endpoint health (with TLS flags) checks each member directly; on kubeadm you also check the etcd static Pod with crictl. componentstatuses is deprecated and needs the API server, and kubeadm's etcd is not a systemd unit.","Troubleshoot cluster components",["componentstatuses is deprecated and needs a working API server, which is the thing failing to reach etcd.","etcd requires client TLS, so a plain HTTP curl to /healthz is refused and does not report health.","kubeadm's etcd is a static Pod, not a systemd unit, so systemctl status etcd shows nothing.",null],2],["cka91",0,5,"metrics-server is Running but not Ready in a kind cluster, and its logs show \"x509: cannot validate certificate ... doesn't contain any IP SANs\" when scraping kubelets. What is the usual lab fix?",["Add --kubelet-insecure-tls to the metrics-server container args (lab clusters only)","Delete the kube-root-ca.crt ConfigMap so it is regenerated","Run kubeadm certs renew all inside the kind node","Switch metrics-server to scrape each kubelet's read-only port 10255 over plain HTTP instead"],0,"kind's kubelet serving certificates are self-signed without IP SANs, so metrics-server cannot verify them. Skipping verification is acceptable in a throwaway lab; in production enable serverTLSBootstrap and approve kubelet serving CSRs instead.","Monitor cluster and application resource usage",[null,"Deleting kube-root-ca.crt concerns the cluster CA bundle, not kubelet serving certs with missing IP SANs.","kubeadm certs renew rotates control-plane certs, not the kind kubelet serving certificates causing this scrape error.","The read-only port 10255 is deprecated and disabled by default; metrics-server does not scrape it."],2],["x1",0,1,"Your team keeps a base and a prod overlay for a monitoring stack, and ./overlays/prod holds a kustomization.yaml. Which command renders the overlay and applies it to the cluster in one step?",["kubectl apply -f ./overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml","kubectl apply -k ./overlays/prod","helm install prod ./overlays/prod","kubectl create -k ./overlays/prod --dry-run=server -o yaml"],1,"kubectl apply -k (built on Kustomize) builds the kustomization in that directory and applies the result. Passing kustomization.yaml to -f is tempting, but that file is build instructions, not an API object.","Domain 1: Kustomize",["A kustomization.yaml is not a Kubernetes object, so applying it with -f fails instead of building the overlay.",null,"Helm expects a chart with Chart.yaml and templates; a Kustomize directory is not a chart.","A server-side dry run only validates and prints the objects; nothing is persisted in the cluster."],1],["x2",0,1,"A CI pipeline uses ServiceAccount build-bot in namespace ci. Before handing it over, you want to confirm it can create Deployments there. Which command checks exactly that identity?",["kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n ci --as=build-bot","kubectl auth whoami --as=system:serviceaccount:ci:build-bot","kubectl get rolebindings -n ci -o wide --as=build-bot","kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n ci --as=system:serviceaccount:ci:build-bot"],3,"ServiceAccounts authenticate as system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<name>, and auth can-i with --as evaluates RBAC for that identity. Using --as=build-bot is the tempting shortcut, but it checks a different, user-type subject.","Domain 1: RBAC and kubectl auth can-i",["--as=build-bot impersonates a user literally named build-bot, not the ServiceAccount, so the answer can be misleading.","auth whoami only reports who the request is authenticated as; it says nothing about permissions.","Listing bindings requires reading them yourself and still misses ClusterRoleBindings and aggregated rules.",null],2],["x3",0,1,"The API server is down and you are on a kubeadm node that runs containerd. Which tool lists the Pod sandboxes and containers through the CRI, the same way kubelet sees them?",["crictl pods and crictl ps -a","docker ps -a","ctr -n default containers list","kubectl get pods -A -o wide --request-timeout=5s"],0,"crictl talks to the runtime over the CRI socket and works without the API server, which makes it the standard node-level debugging tool. ctr is tempting, but it is a low-level containerd client that needs the k8s.io namespace and does not understand Pods.","Domain 1: Extension interfaces (CRI and crictl)",[null,"containerd-based nodes normally have no Docker Engine, and Docker would not show CRI-managed containers anyway.","Kubernetes containers live in containerd's k8s.io namespace, so listing the default namespace shows nothing useful.","kubectl still needs the API server, which is down in this scenario."],1],["x4",0,1,"You install a CRD for Backup objects and create a ClusterRole backup-reader with get/list/watch on backups and the label rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-view: \"true\". What is the effect?",["It becomes the default role for new ServiceAccounts in every namespace","It only takes effect after you create a ClusterRoleBinding named view that points at it","Its rules are merged into the built-in view ClusterRole automatically","Nothing changes until you copy the rules into the view ClusterRole by hand"],2,"The built-in view, edit and admin ClusterRoles use aggregationRule label selectors, and a controller merges the rules of any ClusterRole carrying the matching label. Thinking a binding is still required is tempting, but existing bindings to view pick up the new rules immediately.","Domain 1: RBAC aggregated ClusterRoles",["ServiceAccounts get no roles automatically; labels on a ClusterRole never create bindings.","Aggregation is done by the controller that fills view's rules; no extra binding to the new role is needed.",null,"The aggregation controller copies matching rules automatically, and manual edits to view would be overwritten."],3],["x5",0,1,"You created and approved CertificateSigningRequest jane (signer kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client). How do you save her signed certificate to jane.crt?",["kubectl certificate approve jane --output=jane.crt","kubeadm certs renew jane > jane.crt","kubectl get csr jane -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > jane.crt","Copy /etc/kubernetes/pki/jane.crt from the control plane node"],2,"Once approved, the signer puts the base64-encoded PEM certificate in status.certificate of the CSR, which you extract and decode. Looking in /etc/kubernetes/pki is tempting because cluster certificates live there, but user certificates are never stored on disk by Kubernetes.","Domain 1: Users via CertificateSigningRequests",["certificate approve only sets the Approved condition; it has no option to write the issued certificate to a file.","kubeadm certs renew handles the cluster's own component certificates, not user CSRs.",null,"The signer returns the certificate in the CSR object's status; nothing is written to the pki directory."],2],["x6",0,1,"Jane now has jane.key and a signed jane.crt. What must you add to a kubeconfig so kubectl can act as her against cluster lab?",["set-credentials jane with the cert and key, then a context pairing lab with jane","Run kubectl create user jane --cert=jane.crt, which registers her in the API server","Create a ServiceAccount named jane and attach jane.crt to it as a token Secret","Copy jane.crt into /etc/kubernetes/pki so the API server trusts her from then on"],0,"kubectl config set-credentials adds the user with client-certificate and client-key, and set-context/use-context ties it to a cluster. Creating a ServiceAccount is tempting, but it is a different identity type and cannot carry her certificate.","Domain 1: kubeconfig files and users",[null,"Kubernetes has no User objects; users exist only as names in certificates or tokens, and there is no create user command.","ServiceAccount tokens are a separate mechanism; a client certificate cannot be stored as one.","The API server trusts client certificates signed by its CA; copying her certificate there does nothing."],2],["x7",0,1,"You are about to run kubeadm init for a cluster that will use Flannel. Which kubeadm init flag sets the Pod network range so the controller manager hands each node a podCIDR?",["--service-cidr","--apiserver-advertise-address","--control-plane-endpoint","--pod-network-cidr"],3,"--pod-network-cidr enables per-node podCIDR allocation, which plugins such as Flannel rely on, and must match the CNI configuration. --service-cidr is the tempting mix-up because it is also a CIDR, but it covers virtual Service IPs.","Domain 1: kubeadm init and CNI",["This sets the range for Service ClusterIPs, not the Pod addresses the CNI hands out.","This only picks the IP the local API server advertises to other members.","This sets the shared address for HA API servers and has nothing to do with Pod IPs.",null],1],["x8",0,1,"On a kubeadm control plane node you ran kubeadm certs renew all because certificates were close to expiry. What else must you do for the renewal to take effect?",["Nothing; every control plane component watches its certificate files and reloads them","Restart the control plane static Pods and recopy admin.conf where it is used","Run kubeadm upgrade apply to push the renewed certificates to every node","Approve the renewed certificates with kubectl certificate approve on each one"],1,"kubeadm certs renew rewrites the files, but the running kube-apiserver, controller manager, scheduler and etcd keep the old ones until restarted, and a kubeconfig copied from admin.conf still has the old client cert. Assuming automatic reload is the tempting mistake.","Domain 1: kubeadm certificates",["The docs require restarting the control plane Pods after renewal; they do not all hot-reload their serving certificates.",null,"upgrade apply upgrades versions; renewal is per control plane node and nothing is pushed to workers.","kubeadm signs these with the cluster CA directly; no CSR objects are created for them."],3],["x9",0,1,"A CRD lists two versions: v1alpha1 (served: true, storage: false) and v1 (served: true, storage: true). What does storage: true on v1 mean?",["v1 is the only version clients can read and write through the API","v1 objects are saved on a PersistentVolume created by the operator","Both versions are written to etcd side by side for every object","They are stored in etcd as v1, whichever version clients use"],3,"In a CRD, served controls which versions the API exposes and exactly one version has storage: true, the form written to etcd. Reading it as the only usable version is tempting, but that is what served controls.","Domain 1: CustomResourceDefinitions",["Both versions have served: true, so clients can use either one through the API.","Custom resources are stored in etcd by the API server; no volume is involved.","Exactly one version may be the storage version; objects are stored once in that version.",null],2],["x10",0,1,"The chart's values.yaml sets replicaCount: 1, prod.yaml sets replicaCount: 3, and you run helm upgrade --install web ./chart -f prod.yaml --set replicaCount=5. How many replicas are rendered?",["3","5","1","The command fails because replicaCount is set twice"],1,"Helm gives --set the highest precedence, then -f files, then the chart's values.yaml. The -f value of 3 is the tempting answer, but --set wins when both set the same key.","Domain 1: Helm values",["Values from -f files override chart defaults but are themselves overridden by --set.",null,"Chart defaults have the lowest precedence and are overridden by both -f and --set.","Helm merges values in order of precedence; setting the same key in several places is normal."],2],["x11",0,1,"After helm upgrade of release ingress in namespace ingress broke the controller, you must return to the last working release. What is the Helm-native way?",["kubectl rollout undo deployment --all -n ingress","helm uninstall ingress, then install it again from scratch","helm history ingress -n ingress, then helm rollback ingress <revision> -n ingress","helm repo update, then helm upgrade ingress --previous"],2,"helm history lists release revisions and helm rollback redeploys a chosen one, keeping Helm's records in sync. kubectl rollout undo is tempting because it reverts Pods, but it ignores ConfigMaps and other chart objects and confuses the next Helm upgrade.","Domain 1: Helm",["This reverts Deployments behind Helm's back, so Helm's release record and other objects stay on the broken revision.","Uninstalling removes all release resources and causes an outage; rollback restores the old revision in place.",null,"There is no --previous flag, and updating the repo only refreshes the chart index."],1],["x12",0,1,"A Kustomize prod overlay must run image registry.local/api at tag 2.4.0 instead of the base's tag, without editing the base manifests. Which kustomization.yaml field is meant for this?",["images, with name registry.local/api and newTag 2.4.0","configMapGenerator with a key named image","commonLabels with image: registry.local/api:2.4.0","namePrefix: v2-4-0- so the new tag is picked up by name"],0,"The images transformer rewrites the tag (or name or digest) of matching container images across all resources. commonLabels is the tempting distractor because it also applies everywhere, but it only edits metadata labels.","Domain 1: Kustomize",[null,"A generated ConfigMap only produces data for Pods; it never changes container images.","commonLabels adds labels and selectors, it does not touch the image field.","namePrefix only renames resources; the containers keep their original images."],2],["x13",0,1,"A disk replacement on worker node03 is planned for later today. Right now you only need to stop new Pods from being placed there, and running Pods must stay put. Which command?",["kubectl cordon node03","kubectl drain node03 --ignore-daemonsets","kubectl taint nodes node03 maint=true:NoExecute","kubectl delete node node03"],0,"cordon marks the node unschedulable and leaves existing Pods alone; drain is cordon plus eviction. drain is tempting because it is the usual maintenance command, but it moves the Pods immediately.","Domain 1: Node maintenance",[null,"drain also evicts the running Pods, which is more than the task asks for right now.","A NoExecute taint evicts Pods that do not tolerate it, so running workloads would move.","Deleting the Node object removes it from the cluster and its Pods get cleaned up."],1],["x14",0,1,"Your team plans an external etcd cluster and wants the best fault tolerance for the number of machines it buys. Why are 3 or 5 members preferred over 4?",["etcd refuses to start a cluster that has an even number of members","With four members two leaders can be elected at the same time, causing split-brain","Four members need three for quorum, so they tolerate one failure, like three","kubeadm only lets you join odd numbers of control plane nodes"],2,"Quorum is a majority: floor(n/2)+1. Three members tolerate one failure, four still tolerate one, five tolerate two, so an even member adds cost without resilience. The idea that etcd rejects even counts is tempting but false.","Domain 1: HA control plane and etcd quorum",["etcd runs with an even member count; it is just no more fault tolerant than the odd count below it.","Raft needs a majority (three of four) to elect a leader, so two simultaneous leaders cannot happen.",null,"kubeadm has no such rule; the guidance comes from quorum math, not from a tool limit."],3],["x15",0,1,"kube-apiserver in a cluster runs 1.35. Under the Kubernetes version skew policy, which kubelet version is NOT allowed on a node?",["1.35","1.36","1.34","1.32"],1,"kubelet must never be newer than kube-apiserver and may be up to three minor versions older. 1.32 is tempting because it looks far behind, but three minors of skew is permitted, while any newer kubelet is not.","Domain 1: Version skew",["A kubelet at the same minor version as the API server is always supported.",null,"kubelet may be older than kube-apiserver, and one minor version older is well within the policy.","The policy allows kubelet to be up to three minor versions older than kube-apiserver, so 1.32 is still supported."],2],["x16",0,2,"Deployment web is on revision 5 after several bad releases. You want to return to revision 2 specifically. Which commands do this?",["kubectl rollout restart deployment/web --revision=2","kubectl apply -f web.yaml --revision=2","kubectl scale deployment/web --replicas=2","kubectl rollout history deployment/web, then kubectl rollout undo deployment/web --to-revision=2"],3,"rollout history shows the stored revisions and rollout undo --to-revision rolls the template back to that one. rollout restart is tempting because it also triggers a rollout, but it keeps the current template.","Domain 2: Rolling updates and rollbacks",["rollout restart has no revision flag; it only restarts Pods on the current template.","kubectl apply does not accept a revision; it applies whatever the file contains.","This changes the replica count, not the Pod template revision.",null],1],["x17",0,2,"Deployment api has replicas: 4 with strategy RollingUpdate, maxSurge: 1 and maxUnavailable: 0. How does a rollout to a new image proceed?",["All four old Pods are removed first and four new ones are created afterwards","Available Pods can drop to 3 at a time while each old Pod is replaced","Up to 5 Pods exist; an old Pod goes only after a new one is ready","The rollout stalls, since maxUnavailable: 0 leaves no room for any change"],2,"maxSurge caps extra Pods above replicas and maxUnavailable caps how many can be missing; with 1/0 the controller surges one new Pod, waits for readiness, then scales old down. Thinking the rollout stalls is tempting, but the surge makes progress possible.","Domain 2: Deployment rolling updates",["That is the Recreate strategy; RollingUpdate replaces Pods gradually.","maxUnavailable: 0 forbids going below 4 available Pods during the update.",null,"maxSurge: 1 gives the room: a new Pod is created above the desired count first."],2],["x18",0,2,"Namespace team-a has a ResourceQuota on requests.cpu and requests.memory. Many existing manifests set no requests, and those Pods are now rejected. What fixes this without editing every manifest?",["Raise the ResourceQuota's requests.cpu and requests.memory","A PriorityClass with globalDefault: true for the namespace","A HorizontalPodAutoscaler for each Deployment","A LimitRange in team-a that sets defaultRequest (and default limits) for CPU and memory"],3,"A LimitRange admission plugin fills in default requests and limits for containers that set none, so the quota can account for them. Raising the quota is tempting, but the rejection is about missing requests, not about running out of quota.","Domain 2: LimitRange and ResourceQuota",["A quota on requests forces every Pod to declare them; raising the totals does not add requests to Pods that lack them.","Priority affects scheduling order and preemption, not the requests the quota needs.","An HPA scales replicas; it cannot add resource requests to rejected Pods.",null],3],["x19",0,2,"A Pod carries the toleration key node.kubernetes.io/unreachable, operator Exists, effect NoExecute, tolerationSeconds: 300. Its node loses contact with the control plane. What happens?",["It stays bound for 300 seconds, then it is evicted","The Pod is evicted immediately because the node is unreachable","The Pod is never evicted because it tolerates the taint","The Pod is moved 300 seconds after the node comes back"],0,"When a node becomes unreachable, the node lifecycle controller adds a NoExecute taint; a toleration with tolerationSeconds keeps the Pod bound only for that long. Reading the toleration as permanent protection is the tempting error.","Domain 2: Taints and tolerations (NoExecute)",[null,"The toleration exists to delay that eviction; only Pods without it are evicted at once.","tolerationSeconds limits how long the toleration holds; without it the Pod would stay forever.","Eviction is timed from when the taint is added, not from recovery."],2],["x20",0,2,"You want the scheduler to avoid node spare1 when other nodes have room, but still use it if nothing else fits. Which taint effect does this?",["NoSchedule","PreferNoSchedule","NoExecute","SoftNoSchedule"],1,"PreferNoSchedule is a soft taint the scheduler tries to honour. NoSchedule is tempting because it sounds similar, but it blocks untolerated Pods completely.","Domain 2: Taints and tolerations",["NoSchedule is a hard rule: Pods without a toleration are never placed there.",null,"NoExecute also evicts running Pods that do not tolerate it, which is far stronger.","There is no SoftNoSchedule effect; the soft version is called PreferNoSchedule."],1],["x21",0,2,"Pod A mounts key app.conf of ConfigMap web-cfg with subPath. Pod B mounts the whole web-cfg as a volume. After you edit the ConfigMap, Pod B sees the change a little later but Pod A never does. Why?",["The ConfigMap was created with immutable: true","Files mounted with subPath do not receive ConfigMap updates","kubelet's ConfigMap sync loop is disabled on the node where Pod A runs","Pod A reads app.conf through environment variables"],1,"kubelet refreshes projected ConfigMap volumes over time, but a subPath mount is bound once and never updated, so the Pod must be restarted. Blaming env vars is tempting because they also ignore updates, but that is not how Pod A consumes it.","Domain 2: ConfigMaps",["An immutable ConfigMap could not have been edited, and Pod B would not see a change either.",null,"Nothing points to a node problem; subPath behaviour explains the difference fully.","The scenario says Pod A mounts the key as a file, not as an env var."],3],["x22",0,2,"Namespace batch has a ResourceQuota of pods: 10. You scale Deployment worker to 12 replicas while nothing else runs there. What do you observe?",["10 Pods run; the ReplicaSet logs FailedCreate for exceeded quota","12 Pods are created and 2 stay Pending with FailedScheduling events","The kubectl scale command is rejected and the Deployment keeps its old count","The two oldest Pods are evicted to make room for the new ones"],0,"ResourceQuota is an admission check: Pod creation beyond the limit is refused, and the ReplicaSet controller logs FailedCreate while it keeps retrying. Pending Pods with FailedScheduling are tempting, but that pattern belongs to node capacity, not quota.","Domain 2: ResourceQuota",[null,"Quota is enforced at admission, so the extra Pods are never created and never reach the scheduler.","The Deployment object itself is not counted by the pods quota, so scaling succeeds.","ResourceQuota never evicts running Pods; it only rejects new ones."],2],["x23",0,2,"StatefulSet db has 3 replicas and the default Pod management policy. db-0 is Running but fails its readiness probe, and db-1 never appears. Why?",["StatefulSets wait until all PVCs for every replica are bound before creating any Pod","The headless Service named in serviceName has no endpoints yet","podManagementPolicy: Parallel is the default and it limits Pods to one at a time","OrderedReady creates each Pod only after the previous one is Running and Ready"],3,"With the default OrderedReady policy the controller creates db-0, waits for Running and Ready, then db-1 and so on; an unready db-0 blocks the rest. The headless Service is tempting because it is required, but it does not gate creation.","Domain 2: StatefulSets",["PVCs are created per Pod as it is created; db-0 already exists, so this is not the blocker.","The governing Service provides DNS; its endpoints do not gate Pod creation.","OrderedReady is the default, and Parallel would create all Pods at once.",null],2],["x24",0,2,"A batch team wants its Pods placed ahead of normal Pods in the scheduling queue, but they must never evict running Pods to make room. What should their PriorityClass set?",["A low value with globalDefault: true","A high value with preemptionPolicy: PreemptLowerPriority","A high value with preemptionPolicy: Never","A high value, plus a PodDisruptionBudget on every other workload"],2,"preemptionPolicy: Never keeps the queue priority from value but disables preemption. Adding PDBs is tempting, but preemption respects them only on a best-effort basis, so it is not a guarantee.","Domain 2: PriorityClasses and preemption",["globalDefault makes it apply to Pods with no class and a low value puts them behind others.","That is the default and allows evicting lower-priority Pods, which the team must avoid.",null,"PDBs guard voluntary disruptions such as drain; scheduler preemption does not guarantee to respect them."],3],["x25",0,3,"StatefulSet db in namespace data uses serviceName db-hs, a headless Service. Which DNS name reaches only Pod db-0?",["db-0.data.svc.cluster.local","db-hs.db-0.data.svc.cluster.local","db-0.data.pod.cluster.local","db-0.db-hs.data.svc.cluster.local"],3,"A headless Service gives StatefulSet Pods stable records of the form <pod>.<service>.<namespace>.svc.<zone>. Dropping the Service part is tempting, but then the name has no record.","Domain 3: Headless Services and DNS",["Per-Pod names sit under the governing Service name, which is missing here.","The labels are reversed; the Pod's hostname comes before the Service name.","The pod zone uses dashed IP addresses, not Pod names, and is not how StatefulSet Pods are addressed.",null],2],["x26",0,3,"Pods must reach an outside database at db.corp.example using the in-cluster name legacy-db, so the target can change later without touching the apps. Which Service type fits?",["NodePort pointing at port 5432","Headless with a selector matching app=legacy-db","ExternalName with externalName: db.corp.example","LoadBalancer with loadBalancerIP: db.corp.example"],2,"ExternalName makes CoreDNS answer the Service name with a CNAME to the outside host. A headless Service is tempting because it also skips a ClusterIP, but with a selector it only returns Pod addresses.","Domain 3: Service types (ExternalName)",["NodePort exposes cluster Pods outward; it does not alias an outside host.","A selector-based headless Service lists cluster Pods, and there are none for an external host.",null,"LoadBalancer requests an external address for cluster Pods; it does not alias an outside DNS name."],1],["x27",0,3,"A legacy database runs on two VMs at 10.0.5.10 and 10.0.5.11, port 5432. Pods should use a normal ClusterIP Service named pgdb that load balances between them. How?",["An ExternalName Service whose externalName is 10.0.5.10,10.0.5.11","Create pgdb with no selector, plus an EndpointSlice labeled kubernetes.io/service-name: pgdb listing both IPs","A Service whose selector matches labels on the nodes hosting the VMs","A NetworkPolicy ipBlock allowing 10.0.5.0/24 on port 5432"],1,"Without a selector, Kubernetes does not manage endpoints, so you add an EndpointSlice tied to the Service by the kubernetes.io/service-name label and kube-proxy balances across it. ExternalName is tempting, but it only works as a DNS alias.","Domain 3: Services without selectors and EndpointSlices",["externalName must be a DNS name, not a list of IPs, and it would not give a ClusterIP.",null,"Service selectors match Pods, never Nodes or external machines.","A NetworkPolicy only filters traffic; it creates no Service or endpoints."],3],["x28",0,3,"After applying a default-deny egress NetworkPolicy in namespace shop, apps can still reach allowed IPs by address, but every name lookup fails. What must you add?",["Egress to the CoreDNS Pods on UDP and TCP port 53","An ingress rule on the CoreDNS Pods allowing traffic from namespace shop","Restart CoreDNS so it picks up the new NetworkPolicy","Set dnsPolicy: Default on every Pod in shop"],0,"Default-deny egress blocks DNS traffic too, so policies usually add an egress rule to kube-dns on port 53 for both UDP and TCP. An ingress rule on CoreDNS is tempting, but the traffic is dropped leaving the client namespace.","Domain 3: NetworkPolicy egress",[null,"Ingress to kube-system is not restricted here; the block is shop's own egress.","CoreDNS is healthy; the packets never leave the shop Pods.","That sends queries to the node's resolvers, which egress still blocks, and loses cluster names."],2],["x29",0,3,"A NetworkPolicy on db Pods has this ingress rule:\n\nfrom:\n- namespaceSelector:\n    matchLabels: {team: web}\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels: {app: frontend}\n\nWhich sources are allowed?",["Only Pods labeled app=frontend that run in namespaces labeled team=web","All Pods in team=web namespaces, plus app=frontend Pods in the policy's namespace","Only app=frontend Pods in the policy's own namespace","Nothing, because one peer cannot combine the two selectors"],0,"Inside one from entry, namespaceSelector and podSelector are ANDed; written as two dash items they are ORed. The OR reading is the tempting trap because the YAML differs by a single dash.","Domain 3: NetworkPolicy selectors",[null,"That is the meaning when the two selectors are separate list items (OR); here they are in one item (AND).","The namespaceSelector widens the search to team=web namespaces instead of the local one.","Combining namespaceSelector and podSelector in one peer is valid and means both must match."],3],["x30",0,3,"A cluster uses Flannel alone as its CNI. You apply a default-deny ingress NetworkPolicy, but Pods still reach each other. What is the explanation?",["The policy needs policyTypes: [Egress] as well before it applies","Existing Pods must be restarted before a new policy applies to them","NetworkPolicies filter Service traffic only, not direct Pod IPs","Flannel alone does not enforce NetworkPolicy objects"],3,"The API server stores NetworkPolicies, but the CNI enforces them; Flannel on its own provides connectivity without policy. Restarting Pods is tempting, but no restart helps if nothing enforces the rules.","Domain 3: NetworkPolicy and CNI",["A policy with Ingress in policyTypes and no rules already denies ingress on a supporting CNI.","Enforcing CNIs apply policies to running Pods without restarts.","Policies select Pods and filter by Pod traffic, whether it arrives via a Service or not.",null],2],["x31",0,3,"An Ingress rule has path /api with pathType: Exact pointing at Service api. A request for /api/users returns 404 from the controller. Why?",["Exact matching is case-insensitive and needs a trailing slash","pathType Exact needs a rewrite-target annotation to reach Services","Exact matches only /api itself, not paths below it","Service api must be of type NodePort for Ingress to reach it"],2,"Exact requires the request path to equal the rule's path, while Prefix matches /api and anything under /api/ by path element. A rewrite annotation is tempting because it is common with NGINX, but it does not change which rule matches.","Domain 3: Ingress path types",["Exact is case-sensitive and matches the full path exactly; slashes are not added.","No annotation is needed for a match; the path simply does not match.",null,"Ingress controllers route to ClusterIP Services normally."],2],["x32",0,3,"An Ingress in namespace shop has tls with secretName shop-tls, but the controller serves its default certificate. shop-tls exists only in namespace ingress-nginx. What is wrong?",["The Secret must be of type Opaque rather than kubernetes.io/tls","The Secret must be in the Ingress's own namespace","The backend Service must expose port 443 for TLS to work","The controller must be restarted after every new Ingress"],1,"An Ingress can only reference Secrets in its own namespace, so the controller cannot use shop-tls and falls back to its default certificate. Restarting the controller is tempting, but it would still not see the Secret.","Domain 3: Ingress TLS",["TLS Secrets for Ingress use type kubernetes.io/tls with tls.crt and tls.key.",null,"TLS terminates at the controller; the backend can stay on plain HTTP.","Ingress controllers watch the API and pick up new Ingresses automatically."],2],["x33",0,3,"Using the Gateway API, requests with header x-canary: true must go to Service web-v2 and all others to web-v1. Where is this configured?",["On the Gateway, with a listener hostname of x-canary","In HTTPRoute rules: one with matches.headers for x-canary sending to web-v2, and a default rule to web-v1","In the GatewayClass parametersRef for the controller","As weights of 1 and 0 on the two backendRefs"],1,"HTTPRoute rules can match on headers, paths and query parameters and send each match to its own backendRefs. Weights are tempting because they also split traffic between versions, but they are random and cannot target a header.","Domain 3: Gateway API HTTPRoute matches",["Listeners define ports, protocols and hostnames; they do not match request headers.",null,"GatewayClass picks the implementation; per-request routing lives in routes.","Weights split traffic by percentage, not by header value."],2],["x34",0,3,"On a kubeadm cluster, where do you check which proxy mode (iptables, IPVS or nftables) kube-proxy was configured with?",["The spec of Service kubernetes in the default namespace","The CNI config files in /etc/cni/net.d","The mode field of the kube-proxy ConfigMap","The Allocated resources section of kubectl describe node"],2,"kubeadm deploys kube-proxy as a DaemonSet that reads its KubeProxyConfiguration from the kube-proxy ConfigMap; an empty mode uses the platform default. The CNI directory is tempting, but kube-proxy and the CNI are configured separately.","Domain 3: kube-proxy modes",["That Service fronts the API server and holds no kube-proxy settings.","Those configure the CNI plugin; kube-proxy's mode is separate.",null,"This shows reserved CPU and memory, not networking settings."],1],["x35",0,3,"Which Service fronts the CoreDNS Pods on a kubeadm cluster, and in which namespace?",["Service kube-dns in namespace kube-system","Service coredns in namespace default","Service kubernetes in namespace default","Service cluster-dns in namespace kube-public"],0,"For compatibility, CoreDNS is exposed through Service kube-dns in kube-system, and its ClusterIP is the address kubelet writes into Pods' resolv.conf. The name coredns is tempting because that is the Deployment's name.","Domain 3: CoreDNS",[null,"The Deployment is named coredns, but the Service keeps the historical name kube-dns and is in kube-system.","That Service points at the API server, not at DNS.","No such Service exists; kube-public holds public cluster info, not DNS."],1],["x36",0,3,"A Pod in namespace web needs to call Service api, which lives in namespace backend. The short name api does not resolve. Which name should the app use?",["backend.api","api.svc","api.backend.pod.cluster.local","api.backend (or api.backend.svc.cluster.local)"],3,"A Pod's search path includes its own namespace, so short names only find local Services; other namespaces need <service>.<namespace>. backend.api is the tempting swap of the two labels.","Domain 3: Service DNS names",["The order is service first, then namespace.","The svc label must follow a namespace; api.svc does not name any Service.","The pod zone is for Pod IP records, not Service names.",null],1],["x37",0,4,"A 3-replica Deployment spread over several nodes must write to one shared directory. Its PVC uses a block-storage class with ReadWriteOnce, and the second Pod hangs with a Multi-Attach error. What is the right fix?",["Change the PVC's access mode to ReadOnlyMany so all nodes can mount it","Set the PV's reclaim policy to Retain","Create another PVC with the same name in the same namespace","Use RWX-capable storage, such as NFS or a file-based CSI driver"],3,"ReadWriteOnce block volumes attach to one node at a time, so Pods on other nodes cannot mount them; shared writes need RWX-capable storage. ReadOnlyMany is tempting because it allows many nodes, but the Pods could no longer write.","Domain 4: Access modes",["The Pods need to write, and ReadOnlyMany mounts are read-only.","Reclaim policy only matters after the claim is deleted; it has nothing to do with attaching.","Names are unique per namespace, and a second claim would be a different volume anyway.",null],3],["x38",0,4,"Which PersistentVolume reclaim policy is deprecated, with dynamic provisioning recommended instead?",["Recycle","Retain","Delete","Archive"],0,"Recycle ran a basic scrub (rm -rf) and made the PV available again; it is deprecated in favour of dynamic provisioning. Retain is tempting because it is often avoided in production, but it is not deprecated.","Domain 4: Reclaim policies",[null,"Retain is fully supported and keeps the volume for manual recovery.","Delete is the normal default for dynamically provisioned volumes.","There is no Archive reclaim policy in Kubernetes."],1],["x39",0,4,"In a three-node lab cluster, a Pod using a hostPath PV at /data seems to lose its files whenever it is rescheduled. What is the cause?",["hostPath volumes are backed by tmpfs and cleared on every restart","hostPath is node-local, so another node has a different /data","The Delete reclaim policy wipes the directory each time the Pod restarts","ReadWriteOnce allows only one write before the volume becomes read-only"],1,"A hostPath PV has no node pinning by itself, so the scheduler can place the Pod anywhere and it mounts that node's /data. The reclaim policy is tempting to blame, but it only acts when the PVC is deleted.","Domain 4: hostPath and static PVs",["hostPath mounts a real directory on the node's filesystem; it is not memory-backed by default.",null,"Reclaim policies act when the claim is deleted, not when a Pod restarts.","RWO limits attachment to one node; it does not limit the number of writes."],2],["x40",0,4,"A cluster has a default StorageClass. You pre-created PV legacy-pv with no storageClassName and want a new PVC to bind to it rather than trigger dynamic provisioning. What must the PVC set?",["Nothing: leave storageClassName out so it matches the PV's empty class","volumeBindingMode: Immediate in the PVC spec","storageClassName: \"\" explicitly (plus matching size and access mode)","accessModes: [ReadWriteOncePod] so it can only bind statically"],2,"An explicit empty string means no class, which matches a PV without a class; omitting the field lets the DefaultStorageClass admission plugin add the default. Omitting the field is the tempting answer because it looks like no class.","Domain 4: PV and PVC binding",["An omitted field gets the default class filled in by admission, so it no longer matches the classless PV.","volumeBindingMode is a StorageClass field, not a PVC field.",null,"Access mode does not decide between static and dynamic binding."],3],["x41",0,4,"StatefulSet cache should delete its PVCs automatically when the StatefulSet itself is deleted, but keep them when it is scaled down. What do you configure?",["reclaimPolicy: Delete on the StorageClass","podManagementPolicy: Parallel","persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy with whenDeleted: Delete and whenScaled: Retain","An ownerReference from each Pod to its PVC"],2,"The StatefulSet PVC retention policy controls what happens to claims from volumeClaimTemplates on delete and on scale-down. StorageClass reclaimPolicy is tempting, but it acts on PVs after claims are removed, not on the claims.","Domain 4: StatefulSet volumeClaimTemplates",["This deletes a PV once its PVC is gone, but StatefulSet PVCs are not deleted in the first place.","This only changes the order in which Pods are created and removed.",null,"Pods are recreated often; tying PVCs to them would delete data on every Pod replacement."],2],["x42",0,4,"A new PVC stays Pending. Its StorageClass names a CSI provisioner, and the events say it is waiting for the external provisioner to create a volume. Other StorageClasses work. What should you check?",["Whether the worker nodes still carry a storage-related NoSchedule taint","The CSI driver's controller Pods are installed and running","That the PVC uses ReadWriteOncePod, which CSI requires","That kube-proxy is running on the control plane"],1,"With CSI, an external-provisioner sidecar in the driver's controller Pod watches PVCs and creates volumes; if it is missing or failing, claims wait forever. Checking access modes is tempting, but the event points to the missing provisioner.","Domain 4: CSI drivers and dynamic provisioning",["The event points at the provisioner, and node taints never stop a CSI provisioner from creating a volume.",null,"CSI drivers support several access modes; RWOP is not required.","kube-proxy handles Service traffic; it plays no part in provisioning."],2],["x43",0,5,"A Pod is in ImagePullBackOff. kubectl describe shows \"pull access denied ... may require authorization\" for an image in the company's private registry. What fixes it?",["Raise the container's memory limit","Set restartPolicy: Always so kubelet keeps retrying","Run crictl rmi --prune on the node","Create a docker-registry Secret with the credentials and reference it in imagePullSecrets"],3,"The registry is refusing an anonymous pull, so the Pod needs a kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson Secret listed in imagePullSecrets (or on its ServiceAccount). Retrying is tempting, but without credentials every retry fails the same way.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting Pods",["Memory limits affect running containers; the image has not even been downloaded.","kubelet already retries with backoff; the pull will keep failing without credentials.","Pruning deletes unused local images and does nothing about registry authorization.",null],2],["x44",0,5,"Pod api is in CrashLoopBackOff and its current container has not written anything yet. How do you read the output of the container instance that just crashed?",["kubectl logs api --previous","kubectl logs api -f","kubectl get events --previous","kubectl describe pod api --logs"],0,"--previous (-p) returns the logs of the last terminated container instance, which is where the crash message is. Following with -f is tempting, but it attaches to the new, empty instance.","Domain 5: Container output streams",[null,"Following streams the current container, which has not logged anything yet.","get events has no --previous flag and events do not hold container output.","describe has no --logs option; it shows state and events, not stdout."],1],["x45",0,5,"A container restarts repeatedly. kubectl describe shows Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137. What is the most direct remedy?",["Raise the container's memory limit, or fix the app's memory use","Free disk space on the node because of DiskPressure","Increase the CPU limit, since CPU throttling kills the container","Lengthen the liveness probe timeout"],0,"OOMKilled means the container exceeded its memory limit (or the node ran out) and the kernel killed it, giving exit 137. The liveness probe is tempting because it also restarts containers, but its events and reason are different.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting Pods",[null,"Disk pressure causes Pod evictions, not an OOMKilled container reason.","CPU limits throttle but never kill a container.","A liveness failure shows a different restart reason; OOMKilled means the kernel killed it for memory."],2],["x46",0,5,"kubectl logs web-7d9 fails with \"a container name must be specified\". How do you read the output?",["kubectl logs deployment/web-7d9","kubectl logs web-7d9 --previous","kubectl logs web-7d9 -c <container>, or --all-containers","kubectl describe pod web-7d9 --container"],2,"The Pod has several containers, so kubectl needs -c to choose one or --all-containers for all of them. --previous is tempting after a crash, but it does not solve the ambiguity.","Domain 5: Container output streams",["The name is a Pod, and the error is about picking a container, not the resource type.","--previous changes which instance is read but still needs a container name.",null,"describe has no container flag and does not print logs."],1],["x47",0,5,"Pod app shows Init:0/1 for ten minutes. Its init container wait-db loops on nslookup db until it resolves. What is the first useful command?",["kubectl logs app","kubectl rollout restart deployment/app","kubectl top pod app","kubectl logs app -c wait-db"],3,"Init containers must finish before app containers start, and their logs are read with -c <init name>. Plain kubectl logs is tempting, but it targets the main container that is still waiting.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting Pods",["Without -c this targets the app container, which has not started yet.","Restarting creates new Pods that hit the same wait without telling you why.","Resource usage does not explain why a name lookup keeps failing.",null],2],["x48",0,5,"Service web's EndpointSlice lists ready Pod IPs on port 80, but curl to the Service gets \"connection refused\". The containers listen on 8080. What do you change?",["Fix the Service selector so it matches the Pod labels","Set the Service's targetPort to 8080","Remove the NetworkPolicy in the namespace","Restart kube-proxy on every node"],1,"Traffic is forwarded to the endpoint port taken from targetPort, and nothing listens on 80, so the kernel refuses the connection. The selector is the tempting suspect, but endpoints would be empty if it were wrong.","Domain 5: Service troubleshooting",["Ready endpoints are listed, which proves the selector already matches.",null,"A blocking policy usually causes timeouts, and nothing points to one here.","kube-proxy is forwarding correctly; it forwards to the wrong port because of the Service spec."],2],["x49",0,5,"A task asks for each Pod's name and the node it runs on, as two labelled columns. Which command gives that?",["kubectl get pods -o wide --columns=name,node","kubectl get pods -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName","kubectl get pods -o jsonpath='{.items[*].NAME}'","kubectl top pods --containers"],1,"custom-columns takes HEADER:jsonpath pairs, and .spec.nodeName holds the node. -o wide is tempting because it shows the node, but it prints many extra columns and takes no column selection.","Domain 5: Output handling",["There is no --columns flag; -o wide prints a fixed set of columns.",null,"Field names are lowercase paths like .metadata.name; NAME does not exist.","top shows CPU and memory usage, not node placement."],1],["x50",0,5,"On a new admin workstation, kubectl get nodes prints \"The connection to the server localhost:8080 was refused\". What does this usually mean?",["The API server crashed and has fallen back to its insecure port","etcd is down, so the API server refuses connections","kube-proxy is not running on the workstation","No kubeconfig was found, so set KUBECONFIG or copy admin.conf"],3,"When no kubeconfig is found, kubectl falls back to localhost:8080, which nothing serves. Blaming a crashed API server is tempting, but a real outage would show the configured address, usually on 6443.","Domain 5: Cluster access problems",["kubeadm API servers listen on 6443 and have no insecure port; localhost:8080 is kubectl's fallback.","An etcd problem shows errors from the real API server address, not localhost:8080.","kubectl talks to the API server directly and does not use kube-proxy.",null],2],["x51",0,5,"kubectl top nodes returns \"error: Metrics API not available\". What is the most likely cause?",["kubelet is down on every node","The kubeconfig lacks a metrics context","metrics-server is not installed or not running","kube-proxy is in IPVS mode"],2,"kubectl top reads the metrics.k8s.io API, which metrics-server provides through API aggregation. A kubelet outage is tempting, but it would show as NotReady nodes rather than a missing API.","Domain 5: Resource monitoring",["Nodes would show NotReady; the error names the missing Metrics API.","There is no separate metrics context; top uses the normal API server connection.",null,"The proxy mode does not affect whether the Metrics API is served."],1],["x52",0,5,"Pod db stays Pending with the event \"pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims\". What is the next step?",["kubectl describe pvc for that claim to see why it is not bound","Remove taints from the worker nodes","Give the Pod a higher PriorityClass","Restart the kube-scheduler static Pod"],0,"With Immediate binding the claim must be bound before scheduling, so the PVC's events explain the real problem, such as no matching PV or a missing StorageClass. Removing taints is tempting for Pending Pods, but that is not what this event says.","Domain 5: Pending Pods",[null,"The message names the claim, not a taint; the scheduler cannot place the Pod until the claim binds.","Priority cannot bind a volume, and preemption would not help.","The scheduler is doing its job by reporting the unbound claim."],2],["x53",0,5,"kubectl rollout status deployment/web ends with \"exceeded its progress deadline\". The new ReplicaSet's Pods are in ImagePullBackOff due to a tag typo, while the old Pods still serve. What restores the last good state cleanly?",["Delete the new ReplicaSet with kubectl delete rs","Scale the Deployment to 0 and back up","kubectl rollout undo deployment/web","Delete the failing Pods so they are retried"],2,"rollout undo resets the Deployment's Pod template to the previous revision, so the controller scales the good ReplicaSet back and the bad one down. Deleting the new ReplicaSet is tempting, but the Deployment recreates it from its template.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting workloads",["The Deployment still has the bad template and immediately recreates the ReplicaSet.","Scaling keeps the broken template, so all Pods would come back with the bad tag.",null,"Replacements use the same bad image tag and fail the same way."],3],["x54",0,5,"You need all events related to Pod web-0 in namespace shop. Which command filters them?",["kubectl get events -n shop --field-selector involvedObject.name=web-0","kubectl get events -n shop -l pod=web-0","kubectl logs web-0 -n shop --events","kubectl get pod web-0 -n shop -o events"],0,"Events reference their subject in involvedObject, which supports field selectors. A label selector is tempting, but events do not carry the Pod's labels.","Domain 5: Events",[null,"Events are not labelled with the Pod name, so a label selector finds nothing.","kubectl logs has no --events flag; logs are container output.","events is not an output format."],1],["x55",0,5,"A Pod stays Pending with no events at all, while other new Pods in the same namespace schedule normally. What should you check first?",["Whether kube-scheduler is down","spec.schedulerName, in case it names a scheduler that is not running","Taints on the worker nodes","The namespace ResourceQuota"],1,"A Pod that asks for a non-existent scheduler is ignored by the default one, so it stays Pending with no events. A scheduler outage is tempting, but it would stop every new Pod, not just this one.","Domain 5: Pending Pods",["Other new Pods schedule fine, so the default scheduler is working.",null,"The default scheduler would log a FailedScheduling event for taints.","Quota rejections stop the Pod from being created at all, so it would not exist as Pending."],3],["x56",0,5,"A new Pod shows CreateContainerConfigError, and kubectl describe says secret \"db-cred\" not found. What fixes it?",["Add imagePullSecrets for the registry","Restart kubelet on the node","Add a toleration so the Pod can move to another node","Create Secret db-cred in the Pod's namespace"],3,"kubelet cannot build the container config because a referenced Secret is missing; once it exists, kubelet retries and starts the container. imagePullSecrets is tempting because it also mentions secrets, but it only covers registry credentials.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting Pods",["The image is fine; the failure is about a missing Secret the container config needs.","kubelet reports the problem correctly and retries on its own once the Secret exists.","The Pod is already scheduled, and a missing Secret is not node-specific.",null],2],["x57",0,5,"A Java service needs about 90 seconds to start. Its liveness probe starts after 10 seconds and fails three times 10 seconds apart, so the container is killed before it ever finishes starting. What is the best fix?",["Replace the liveness probe with a readiness probe only","Set restartPolicy: Never on the Pod","Lower the liveness probe's periodSeconds to 1","Add a startupProbe that allows enough time to start"],3,"A startupProbe suspends liveness and readiness checks until the app has started, then liveness protects it as before. Dropping liveness for readiness is tempting and also stops the kills, but the app loses self-healing for hangs.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting workloads",["This avoids the kills but loses automatic restarts if the app hangs later.","Deployments require Always, and a killed container would simply stay dead.","Probing more often makes the failures pile up faster and kills the container sooner.",null],3],["x58",0,5,"New Pods avoid worker3. kubectl describe node shows taint node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure:NoSchedule, which nobody added. What is going on?",["Someone ran kubectl cordon worker3","It follows the node's DiskPressure condition; free space to clear it","Remove it with kubectl taint nodes worker3 node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure-","kube-scheduler adds it after scheduling too many Pods to a node"],1,"Nodes are tainted automatically by condition (taint-nodes-by-condition), so the taint follows the DiskPressure condition. Removing the taint by hand is tempting, but it comes back until the real problem is fixed.","Domain 5: Node problems",["cordon sets the unschedulable taint, not a disk-pressure taint.",null,"The node lifecycle controller re-adds the taint while the condition persists.","The scheduler never taints nodes; it only reads taints."],2],["x59",0,5,"You need the recent logs from every Pod of Deployment web (label app=web), with each line showing which Pod wrote it. Which command?",["kubectl logs -l app=web --prefix","kubectl logs deployment/web","kubectl logs web --all-containers","kubectl get events -l app=web --sort-by=.lastTimestamp"],0,"A label selector with -l reads logs from all matching Pods, and --prefix tags each line with its source. kubectl logs deployment/web is tempting because it accepts the Deployment, but it chooses just one Pod.","Domain 5: Container output streams",[null,"This picks one Pod of the Deployment, not all of them.","web is not a Pod name, and --all-containers covers containers of one Pod.","Events describe object changes, not container output."],2],["x60",0,5,"For a report you must list all PersistentVolumes ordered by capacity. Which command sorts them?",["kubectl get pv --sort-by=capacity","kubectl top pv","kubectl get pv --sort-by=.spec.capacity.storage","kubectl get pv -o jsonpath='{sort(.items[*].spec.capacity)}'"],2,"--sort-by takes a JSONPath to the field, and capacity lives at .spec.capacity.storage. Using the column name CAPACITY is tempting, but kubectl does not accept column names there.","Domain 5: Output handling",["--sort-by takes a JSONPath expression, not a column header.","top only covers nodes and Pods.",null,"kubectl JSONPath has no sort function."],1]]);
